BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Wisdom Watch//Annual Edition//EN
CALSCALE:GREGORIAN
METHOD:PUBLISH
X-WR-CALNAME:Wisdom Watch
X-WR-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260901@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261025T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261025T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nSilence is not the absence of experience. Attentio
 n reveals events we habitually exclude as noise or nothing.\n\nFor Cage\, 
 relinquishing control is itself a designed procedure. Chance and silence a
 re not vagueness\; they are ways of admitting events the composer would ot
 herwise exclude.\n\nQUOTATION\n“I have nothing to say and I am saying it
 .” — John Cage\, Lecture on Nothing\n\nSource: Cage's work on silence 
 and listening\; especially 4′33″ and his writings on sound.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260902@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261026T011200
SUMMARY:Can a game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nA finite game is played to conclude in victor
 y. An infinite game is played so that play can continue. Ask what your pre
 sent activity is trying to preserve: victory or possibility.\n\nCarse trea
 ts rules as tools of a game rather than sacred furniture. In infinite play
 \, even the rules remain answerable to the continuation of play.\n\nSource
 : Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260903@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261027T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261027T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nPlaces and things are not simply whole
  or fragmented. Their structure can intensify or diminish the life of the 
 larger field around them.\n\nQUOTATION\nAlexander's earlier phrase “the 
 quality without a name” points toward the later language of degrees of l
 ife.\n\nSource: Alexander's later work on centers and degrees of life\; Th
 e Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260904@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261028T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261028T011200
SUMMARY:Hope contradicts now
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nHope is not merely confidence that present 
 trends will turn out well. It can contradict the apparent finality of the 
 present by orienting life toward what is promised but not yet actual.\n\nM
 oltmann refuses consolation that closes history too soon. Suffering stays 
 real\, but it is denied the right to become the final metaphysical fact.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“Can a game stay open?”): Moltman
 n's future-oriented hope and Carse's infinite play both resist closure. On
 e is theological and eschatological\; the other ludic\, but both ask wheth
 er the future remains genuinely open.\n\nSource: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260905@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261029T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261029T011200
SUMMARY:The margin may be center
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nThe grotesque can disclose realities tha
 t ordinary proportion conceals. Distortion is sometimes a way of making an
  invisible moral structure visible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jürgen Moltmann
  (“Hope contradicts now”): Read today's thought backward through Jürg
 en Moltmann: “Hope contradicts now.” Both distinguish mere ignorance f
 rom the more interesting recognition that a mode of knowing has reached it
 s limit.\n\nSource: O'Connor's fiction and essays on the grotesque and the
  Christian novelist.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260906@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261030T011200
SUMMARY:What resists your map?
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWe notice the content carried by a medium a
 nd overlook the environment the medium creates. Look for the part of exper
 ience your conceptual map makes difficult to perceive.\n\nSource: McLuhan'
 s distinction between media content and the transformations produced by me
 dia themselves.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260907@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261031T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261031T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA crowd can relieve the individual of res
 ponsibility. Becoming a self requires standing personally before the deman
 d one recognizes as true rather than borrowing conviction from numbers.\n\
 nSource: Kierkegaard's writings on 'the single individual' and the crowd.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260908@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261101T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261101T011200
SUMMARY:The wound can be a door
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nWhat the conscious personality rejects may return 
 as symptom\, projection\, or disturbance. The troublesome material can bec
 ome an entrance into a larger self if it is encountered rather than merely
  expelled.\n\nJung treats disturbance as potentially informative. A sympto
 m\, projection\, or dream may be carrying material the conscious attitude 
 has refused to host.\n\nSource: Jung's work on shadow and individuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260909@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261102T011200
SUMMARY:Usefulness has a shadow
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA thing need not justify itself by immediat
 e utility. Imagination\, story\, play\, and beauty may enlarge the person 
 who later decides what counts as useful.\n\nSource: MacDonald's essays on 
 imagination and fairy tales.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260910@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261103T011200
SUMMARY:Less can make more possible
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nGood design does not merely add resources
 . It can reorganize relationships so that less matter and energy support g
 reater capability.\n\nFuller's favorite kind of elegance is functional: mo
 re capability from less matter\, less friction\, and better organization.\
 n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christopher Alexander (“Wholeness has degrees”):
  Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments div
 erge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps aski
 ng whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.\n\nWith Marshall 
 McLuhan (“What resists your map?”): Both thoughts look past the visibl
 e tool toward the environment it creates. The agreement is structural\; th
 e disagreement may be about whether redesign is enough. Buckminster Fuller
 's route is design science\, systems\, and structural efficiency\; Marshal
 l McLuhan's is media ecology and figure/ground perception.\n\nSource: Full
 er's idea of doing more with less and ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260911@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can unmake wonder
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nIn the divine life\, genuine knowledge does
  not terminate desire. Encounter enlarges the capacity for further encount
 er\; fulfillment can deepen rather than extinguish longing.\n\nCONVERSATIO
 N\nWith George MacDonald (“Usefulness has a shadow”): Desire colors th
 e image\, while the image educates desire. Each can quietly teach the othe
 r what to want.\n\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“What resists your map?”): R
 ead today's thought backward through Marshall McLuhan: “What resists you
 r map?.” Both distinguish mere ignorance from the more interesting recog
 nition that a mode of knowing has reached its limit.\n\nSource: Gregory's 
 account of endless progress\, often called epektasis\, especially in The L
 ife of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260912@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261105T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261105T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite\n\nNegation can be intellectual
 ly productive. To say what ultimate reality is not can loosen concepts tha
 t have quietly become substitutes for what they attempt to describe.\n\nCO
 NVERSATION\nWith George MacDonald (“Usefulness has a shadow”): An imag
 e can hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of rema
 ining precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: The Mystical Theology an
 d the apophatic tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260913@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261106T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261106T011200
SUMMARY:What owns your attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nA recurring thought is not merely somethin
 g you think. Repetition can give it a kind of agency over attention and ac
 tion. Observe the thought's pattern before arguing with its content.\n\nHi
 s psychology begins before argument: catch the thought while it is organiz
 ing attention\, rather than after its framing has become the whole world.\
 n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Carl Jung (“The wound can be a door”): Jung's co
 mplexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different psychologies
 \, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of autonomy rath
 er than transparent expressions of consciousness.\n\nSource: Evagrius's an
 alysis of logismoi\, recurring tempting or obsessive thought-patterns.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260914@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261107T011200
SUMMARY:A cell can teach
DESCRIPTION:Desert Fathers\, especially Abba Moses and the sayings traditio
 n\n\nRemaining in one place can expose evasions that movement disguises. C
 onstraint sometimes becomes an instrument of perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\n
 With Carl Jung (“The wound can be a door”): Practice turns identity fr
 om a description into an experiment. Repetition reveals what the self can 
 actually sustain.\n\nSource: The Sayings of the Desert Fathers repeatedly 
 associate staying in one's cell with learning and transformation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260915@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261108T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261108T011200
SUMMARY:Love alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nAttention is not neutral inspection. The quality
  of attention can make another person's reality perceptible without immedi
 ately converting that person into a project\, category\, or instrument.\n\
 nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“What owns your attention?”): B
 oth thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that
  one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filt
 ering perception in the first place. Simone Weil's route is attention\, af
 fliction\, force\, and decreation\; Evagrius Ponticus's is ascetic psychol
 ogy and the analysis of recurring thoughts.\n\nSource: Weil's writings on 
 attention\, affliction\, and love of neighbor.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260916@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261109T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary has depth
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA small\, vulnerable thing can disclose an
  unexpectedly large metaphysical horizon. Scale and significance need not 
 correspond.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Simone Weil (“Love alters perception
 ”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction 
 is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had be
 en filtering perception in the first place. Julian of Norwich's route is v
 isionary theology of creation\, suffering\, and love\; Simone Weil's is at
 tention\, affliction\, force\, and decreation.\n\nSource: Julian's vision 
 of a small object 'the quantity of a hazelnut' held in her hand\, used to 
 contemplate creation and divine love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260917@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nSpiritual and intellectual possession can be
 come an obstacle. Detachment includes relinquishing cherished images of tr
 uth when those images prevent encounter with what exceeds them.\n\nCONVERS
 ATION\nWith Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite (“Remove one certainty”): 
 Eckhart's detachment and Dionysius's negation share an apophatic disciplin
 e: even a true concept can become false in use when held as possession.\n\
 nSource: Eckhart's sermons on detachment\, poverty of spirit\, and knowing
  God beyond images.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260918@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261111T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nAt the limits of ordinary comparison\, oppo
 sitions generated by finite concepts may cease to behave as oppositions. C
 ontradiction can sometimes mark the boundary of a conceptual instrument ra
 ther than the boundary of reality.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Pseudo-Dionysius 
 the Areopagite (“Remove one certainty”): Cusa inherits the apophatic l
 esson that precision about the infinite includes precision about the limit
 s of finite concepts.\n\nSource: Cusa's 'coincidence of opposites' and lea
 rned ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260919@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nBeauty need not be an escape from dif
 ficult making. The finished image can conceal revision\, discipline\, fail
 ure\, and return\; enchantment and workmanship are not opposites.\n\nCONVE
 RSATION\nWith Nicholas of Cusa (“Opposites can coincide”): An image ca
 n hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of remainin
 g precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: Rossetti's intertwined pract
 ice of poetry\, painting\, revision\, and reworking.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260920@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261113T011200
SUMMARY:Renunciation makes room
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nRefusal is not always mere deprivation. G
 iving something up can create a form of interior space in which another al
 legiance\, perception\, or desire becomes possible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith 
 Dante Gabriel Rossetti (“Beauty can demand labor”): The Rossettis shar
 e intense symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire satur
 ate the image while Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, 
 waiting\, and renunciation.\n\nSource: A recurring structure in Rossetti's
  devotional poetry\, where desire\, restraint\, loss\, and transformed des
 ire interact.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260921@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261114T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261114T011200
SUMMARY:Is the joke still a joke?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performance can destabilize the boundary betw
 een event and representation. When the audience cannot locate the frame\, 
 its own expectations become part of the material.\n\nKaufman's ambiguity i
 s not simply trickery. By refusing to stabilize the frame\, he turns inter
 pretation into part of the event.\n\nSource: Kaufman's deliberate blurring
  of character\, prank\, performance\, sincerity\, and audience complicity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260923@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261115T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261115T011200
SUMMARY:Do not canonize reality
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nWhat exists deserves attention\, not worshi
 p. Hope keeps actuality from becoming the measure of possibility.\n\nCONVE
 RSATION\nWith Julian of Norwich (“The ordinary has depth”): Moltmann a
 nd Julian let hope resist the apparent finality of suffering\, but neither
  needs to pretend the wound is unreal.\n\nSource: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260924@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261116T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can be social
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSilence can become a form of solidarity when i
 t clears space to perceive suffering without immediately turning it into s
 elf-display.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Meister Eckhart (“Unknow what you kno
 w”): Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspicion 
 of the identity that must constantly possess\, perform\, and defend itself
 .\n\nSource: later writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260925@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261117T011200
SUMMARY:The maker serves the work
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nArt has its own internal demands. The maker
 's sincerity does not excuse a work that has not found its proper form.\n\
 nImage: Think of a craftsman's judgment arriving in the gesture before it 
 arrives in the sentence.\n\nSource: Art and Scholasticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260926@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261118T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261118T011200
SUMMARY:A system can enslave
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nBlake distrusts systems that become substitute
 s for perception\, especially when inherited categories begin dictating wh
 at can be seen.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jürgen Moltmann (“Do not canonize
  reality”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful f
 riction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks wha
 t had been filtering perception in the first place. William Blake's route 
 is visionary poetry\, contraries\, and imagination\; Jürgen Moltmann's is
  eschatological theology and hope.\n\nSource: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260927@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261119T011200
SUMMARY:Let sounds be themselves
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nListening can loosen the impulse to make every sou
 nd serve intention\, expression\, or hierarchy.\n\nCage's move is composit
 ional rather than merely therapeutic: he changes the conditions under whic
 h preference gets to decide what counts.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William Bla
 ke (“A system can enslave”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of not
 icing. The useful friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while
  the other asks what had been filtering perception in the first place. Joh
 n Cage's route is composition and disciplined listening\; William Blake's 
 is visionary poetry\, contraries\, and imagination.\n\nWith Thomas Merton 
 (“Silence can be social”): Merton and Cage both find that silence is n
 ot blank. For Merton it can strip away performed identity\; for Cage it re
 veals the sound-world already happening.\n\nSource: Silence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260928@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261120T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261120T011200
SUMMARY:Anger clouds contemplation
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nAnger is not merely a moral fault\; it can
  alter the field of perception so that certain realities become difficult 
 to see.\n\nStillness for Evagrius is trained clarity. The goal is not blan
 kness\, but freedom from being involuntarily recruited by every mental eve
 nt.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Let sounds be themselves”): Both
  thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that on
 e asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filteri
 ng perception in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic psy
 chology and the analysis of recurring thoughts\; John Cage's is compositio
 n and disciplined listening.\n\nSource: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260929@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261121T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261121T011200
SUMMARY:A story need not explain
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA story can work on perception before the r
 eader can translate its effect into a proposition.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith W
 illiam Blake (“A system can enslave”): MacDonald and Blake refuse to t
 reat imagination as decorative unreality. For both\, imaginative vision ca
 n expose possibilities ordinary habits of perception conceal.\n\nSource: T
 he Fantastic Imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260930@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261122T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261122T011200
SUMMARY:Schooling can hide learning
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nInstitutional certification can become confused 
 with learning itself\, making informal competence socially invisible.\n\nC
 ONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Anger clouds contemplation”): On
 e asks what enters awareness\; the other asks which environment trains tha
 t awareness. The connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\
 n\nSource: Deschooling Society.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261001@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261123T011200
SUMMARY:The joke may be the doubt
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nSometimes the comic event is not a punchline bu
 t the audience's inability to decide whether a joke is occurring.\n\nKaufm
 an's material is often the audience's certainty about what kind of event i
 s occurring.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Let sounds be themselves
 ”): Cage gives up some choices so unplanned sounds can enter. Kaufman su
 rrenders certainty over what the audience thinks is happening. Both design
  a situation rather than simply deliver content.\n\nSource: performance pr
 actice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261002@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261124T011200
SUMMARY:Carry your leaking bag
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nIn a famous saying\, Moses carries a leaking cont
 ainer to dramatize how one's own faults can trail behind unnoticed while j
 udging another.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Anger clouds c
 ontemplation”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The usef
 ul friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks
  what had been filtering perception in the first place. Abba Moses's route
  is desert wisdom\, humility\, and anti-judgment\; Evagrius Ponticus's is 
 ascetic psychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts.\n\nSource: Sayi
 ngs of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261003@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261125T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261125T011200
SUMMARY:Sincerity can be staged
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performance can make sincerity undecidable wi
 thout making it meaningless.\n\nHe treats entertainment's social contract 
 as something that can itself be performed\, broken\, or made visible.\n\nS
 ource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261004@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261126T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261126T011200
SUMMARY:Design can outrun politics
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nSome problems framed as conflicts over sc
 arcity may change when design alters the resource conditions underneath th
 em.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“The joke may be the doubt”): 
 Both thoughts expose the frame of the game. Their difference is whether th
 e emphasis falls on continuing play\, destabilizing the audience\, or worl
 d-making through rules. Buckminster Fuller's route is design science\, sys
 tems\, and structural efficiency\; Andy Kaufman's is performance\, framing
 \, audience complicity\, and undecidability.\n\nWith Ivan Illich (“Schoo
 ling can hide learning”): Fuller trusts redesign to unlock abundance\; I
 llich worries that systems can become counterproductive even when technica
 lly successful. Their disagreement is a useful brake on either thinker alo
 ne.\n\nSource: World Game and design science.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261005@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261127T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261127T011200
SUMMARY:Attention waits empty
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nReal attention does not rush to fill a question 
 with the first available answer.\n\nDecreation is Weil's refusal of the fa
 ntasy that the self should occupy the center of reality.\n\nCONVERSATION\n
 With Abba Moses (“Carry your leaking bag”): Both thoughts alter the co
 nditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks what becomes pe
 rceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perception in the f
 irst place. Simone Weil's route is attention\, affliction\, force\, and de
 creation\; Abba Moses's is desert wisdom\, humility\, and anti-judgment.\n
 \nSource: Reflections on the Right Use of School Studies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261006@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261128T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261128T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nEnchantment and painstaking revision 
 are not opposites\; an apparently immediate image may be the product of re
 peated return.\n\nSource: poetry and painting practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261007@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261129T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261129T011200
SUMMARY:The many can cohere
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nMultiplicity can belong to order witho
 ut requiring sameness.\n\nFor Maximus\, love is epistemic as well as moral
 : distorted relation can become distorted perception.\n\nSource: cosmic th
 eology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261008@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261130T011200
SUMMARY:The grotesque can see
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nDistortion can reveal moral structures t
 hat polite realism leaves comfortably invisible.\n\nImage: O'Connor's mora
 l world often works like a cracked mirror: the distortion is what finally 
 makes the face visible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Abba Moses (“Carry your le
 aking bag”): O'Connor and Abba Moses distrust moral superiority. One use
 s grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the pers
 on who can see everyone else's fault.\n\nWith Simone Weil (“Attention wa
 its empty”): Weil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet
  both distrust the self-protective stories by which a person avoids realit
 y.\n\nSource: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261009@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261201T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness can be passage
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nNot every loss of orientation is mere fail
 ure\; some transitions cannot preserve the old map.\n\nJohn keeps asking w
 hat desire actually seeks after familiar consolation is removed.\n\nSource
 : Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261010@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261202T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261202T011200
SUMMARY:God has a future
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nMoltmann's theology treats God not merely a
 s the eternal ground behind events but as the one toward whose promised fu
 ture history moves.\n\nFor Moltmann\, the future is theologically active i
 n the present. Promise does not merely predict\; it reorganizes what can b
 e attempted now.\n\nSource: The Coming of God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261011@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261203T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261203T011200
SUMMARY:Centers make centers
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA strong center is supported by other 
 centers around it\; coherence is relational rather than isolated.\n\nAlexa
 nder's process is incremental and reparative: make one change\, then ask w
 hether the whole has become more coherent\, more differentiated\, and more
  alive.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Design can outrun pol
 itics”): Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their temper
 aments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander 
 keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.\n\nWith
  Maximus the Confessor (“The many can cohere”): Alexander's wholeness 
 and Maximus's communion share a structural intuition: unity is strongest w
 hen differences are related without being flattened.\n\nSource: The Nature
  of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261012@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261204T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261204T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can contract
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nDesire can create a center by contraction\, pro
 ducing both form and the risk of enclosure.\n\nDistinction: Harmony is not
  flat peace. For Böhme\, it is an order capable of holding real forces in
  tension.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261013@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261205T011200
SUMMARY:Knowledge has plural modes
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nScientific\, philosophical\, artistic\, and
  contemplative knowing need not compete as if every truth must use the sam
 e instrument.\n\nMaritain is a realist about art: poetic intuition is not 
 private whim but a mode of contact with being through making.\n\nCONVERSAT
 ION\nWith Christopher Alexander (“Centers make centers”): Maritain's s
 ense that form can be intrinsically intelligible meets Alexander's insiste
 nce that good form is relational and alive\, not merely imposed from outsi
 de.\n\nSource: Degrees of Knowledge.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261014@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261206T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261206T011200
SUMMARY:Memory is a vast room
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nThe self encounters itself through a memo
 ry it uses constantly but cannot fully survey.\n\nAugustine's self is inti
 mate but not transparent. Memory is closer than almost anything\, yet it e
 xceeds the mind's ability to survey it.\n\nSource: Confessions\, Book X.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261015@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261207T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261207T011200
SUMMARY:Love outlasts the wound
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA wound can remain real without being gran
 ted the final word about the whole.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jürgen Moltmann
  (“God has a future”): Moltmann and Julian let hope resist the apparen
 t finality of suffering\, but neither needs to pretend the wound is unreal
 .\n\nSource: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261016@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261208T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261208T011200
SUMMARY:Attention alters noise
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nThe difference between signal and noise is partly 
 a decision about what deserves attention.\n\nCage keeps shifting attention
  from expression to occurrence. The interesting question is often not 'wha
 t does this mean?' but 'what is actually happening when I stop filtering i
 t?'\n\nSource: writings and compositional practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261017@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261209T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261209T011200
SUMMARY:Distraction is still prayer
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nA wandering mind does not automatically inv
 alidate prayer\; the struggle with distraction can itself reveal how atten
 tion works.\n\nShe is relentlessly empirical about extraordinary experienc
 e: intensity proves little unless the experience bears durable fruit in co
 nduct.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Attention alters noise”): Bot
 h thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that o
 ne asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filter
 ing perception in the first place. Teresa of Ávila's route is interior ar
 chitecture\, prayer\, and discernment\; John Cage's is composition and dis
 ciplined listening.\n\nSource: Way of Perfection.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261018@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261210T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261210T011200
SUMMARY:Speed can consume distance
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nFaster transport can reorganize settlement and e
 xpectation so radically that gains in speed create new forms of compulsory
  travel.\n\nIllich's defense of limits is not nostalgia for deprivation. I
 t is an attempt to protect spaces where ordinary competence and mutual aid
  remain possible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christopher Alexander (“Centers 
 make centers”): Illich's convivial tools and Alexander's living patterns
  both oppose dependence on systems that displace local judgment. Human age
 ncy is part of what a good design must preserve.\n\nSource: Energy and Equ
 ity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261019@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261211T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261211T011200
SUMMARY:Can certainty choose?
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nExistential commitment begins where detac
 hed calculation can no longer make the decision for you.\n\nCONVERSATION\n
 With Augustine of Hippo (“Memory is a vast room”): Augustine's divided
  will and Kierkegaard's analysis of despair both make the self a task rath
 er than a transparent possession.\n\nSource: Concluding Unscientific Posts
 cript.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261020@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261212T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261212T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA place can become more or less alive 
 as relationships among its centers strengthen or weaken.\n\nCONVERSATION\n
 With Jacques Maritain (“Knowledge has plural modes”): Maritain's sense
  that form can be intrinsically intelligible meets Alexander's insistence 
 that good form is relational and alive\, not merely imposed from outside.\
 n\nWith Ivan Illich (“Speed can consume distance”): Illich's convivial
  tools and Alexander's living patterns both oppose dependence on systems t
 hat displace local judgment. Human agency is part of what a good design mu
 st preserve.\n\nQUOTATION\nAlexander's earlier phrase “the quality witho
 ut a name” points toward the later language of degrees of life.\n\nSourc
 e: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261021@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261213T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261213T011200
SUMMARY:Consent is not passivity
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nConsent can name a disciplined receptivity that 
 differs from both domination and mere resignation.\n\nDecreation is Weil's
  refusal of the fantasy that the self should occupy the center of reality.
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Teresa of Ávila (“Distraction is still prayer”
 ): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is 
 that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been 
 filtering perception in the first place. Simone Weil's route is attention\
 , affliction\, force\, and decreation\; Teresa of Ávila's is interior arc
 hitecture\, prayer\, and discernment.\n\nWith John Cage (“Attention alte
 rs noise”): Weil's attention and Cage's listening meet in a disciplined 
 refusal to decide too quickly what matters. Weil makes it ethical\; Cage m
 akes it acoustic.\n\nSource: Gravity and Grace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261022@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261214T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261214T011200
SUMMARY:A freak can clarify norms
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nThe socially anomalous figure can reveal
  how strange the supposedly normal order already is.\n\nO'Connor's grotesq
 ue is diagnostic. Distortion makes visible the spiritual disproportions th
 at respectable realism can politely conceal.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Teresa 
 of Ávila (“Distraction is still prayer”): Both thoughts alter the con
 ditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks what becomes per
 ceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perception in the fi
 rst place. Flannery O’Connor's route is grotesque fiction and a Catholic
  theology of grace\; Teresa of Ávila's is interior architecture\, prayer\
 , and discernment.\n\nWith Simone Weil (“Consent is not passivity”): W
 eil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet both distrust t
 he self-protective stories by which a person avoids reality.\n\nSource: My
 stery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261023@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261215T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261215T011200
SUMMARY:Humility needs discernment
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nSelf-abasement and humility are not
  identical\; humility requires truth rather than theatrical self-contempt.
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Can certainty choose?”): P
 ractice turns identity from a description into an experiment. Repetition r
 eveals what the self can actually sustain.\n\nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261024@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261216T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261216T011200
SUMMARY:Roots are relational
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nHuman beings need meaningful participation in in
 herited and local worlds\, not merely abstract rights or material provisio
 n.\n\nWeil's attention is ethical before it is therapeutic. It asks whethe
 r another reality can be perceived without immediately being appropriated 
 by the self.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“A freak can cla
 rify norms”): Weil's attention and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, ye
 t both distrust the self-protective stories by which a person avoids reali
 ty.\n\nSource: The Need for Roots.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261025@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261217T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261217T011200
SUMMARY:Let the image go
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nDetachment can include relinquishing even sp
 iritually valuable images when they become objects of possession.\n\nEckha
 rt pushes inward until the familiar narrated self is no longer the deepest
  ground of identity.\n\nSource: sermons on detachment.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261026@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261218T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261218T011200
SUMMARY:The dream compensates
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA dream can counterbalance the waking attitude by 
 presenting material consciousness has neglected or exaggerated.\n\nJung tr
 eats disturbance as potentially informative. A symptom\, projection\, or d
 ream may be carrying material the conscious attitude has refused to host.\
 n\nSource: dream psychology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261027@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261219T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261219T011200
SUMMARY:Speed changes institutions
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWhen communication accelerates enough\, ins
 titutions built around delay and distance can change category rather than 
 merely operate faster.\n\nHis basic provocation is ecological: a medium ch
 anges ratios among senses\, habits\, institutions\, and speeds before anyo
 ne debates its content.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“A fr
 eak can clarify norms”): One asks what enters awareness\; the other asks
  which environment trains that awareness. The connection is ecological: pe
 rception has infrastructure.\n\nSource: Understanding Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261028@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261220T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261220T011200
SUMMARY:Less can enable more
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nDesign can reorganize relationships so th
 at less material and energy support greater capability.\n\nHis optimism is
  technological but not merely gadget-minded. He looks for redesigns that c
 hange the resource conditions underneath an apparent conflict.\n\nCONVERSA
 TION\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“Speed changes institutions”): Both thoug
 hts look past the visible tool toward the environment it creates. The agre
 ement is structural\; the disagreement may be about whether redesign is en
 ough. Buckminster Fuller's route is design science\, systems\, and structu
 ral efficiency\; Marshall McLuhan's is media ecology and figure/ground per
 ception.\n\nWith Simone Weil (“Consent is not passivity”): One asks wh
 at enters awareness\; the other asks which environment trains that awarene
 ss. The connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSource
 : ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261029@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261221T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261221T011200
SUMMARY:Patterns are relations
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA useful pattern is not a decorative m
 otif but a recurring relation among forces in a situation.\n\nAlexander's 
 process is incremental and reparative: make one change\, then ask whether 
 the whole has become more coherent\, more differentiated\, and more alive.
 \n\nObservation: Alexander prefers sequences of small repairing moves to t
 he imposition of a finished total design.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminste
 r Fuller (“Less can enable more”): Alexander and Fuller both think rel
 ationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system
  and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form actual
 ly feels more alive.\n\nSource: A Pattern Language.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261030@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261222T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261222T011200
SUMMARY:Solitude has company
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nWithdrawal from social noise does not remo
 ve conflict\; it can make internal voices newly audible.\n\nCONVERSATION\n
 With Syncletica of Alexandria (“Humility needs discernment”): Anthony 
 and Syncletica distrust geographical solutions to interior problems. The d
 esert does not magically remove what the traveler brings.\n\nSource: Life 
 and sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261031@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261223T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261223T011200
SUMMARY:Projection hides ownership
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nWhat appears to belong entirely to another person 
 may partly reveal material the observer has not recognized as their own.\n
 \nFor Jung\, a symbol is alive precisely when it exceeds the explanation c
 urrently available for it.\n\nSource: projection.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261101@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261224T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261224T011200
SUMMARY:Paradox tests thought
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA paradox can expose an assumption so famil
 iar that ordinary formulation leaves it invisible.\n\nWonder\, for Chester
 ton\, begins when existence stops being treated as the default setting of 
 the universe.\n\nSource: essays and apologetics.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261102@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261225T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261225T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nSilence becomes crowded when attention stops decid
 ing in advance which sounds count.\n\nFor Cage\, relinquishing control is 
 itself a designed procedure. Chance and silence are not vagueness\; they a
 re ways of admitting events the composer would otherwise exclude.\n\nCONVE
 RSATION\nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Less can enable more”): One asks wh
 at enters awareness\; the other asks which environment trains that awarene
 ss. The connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nQUOTAT
 ION\n“I have nothing to say and I am saying it.” — John Cage\, Lectu
 re on Nothing\n\nSource: 4′33″ and writings on silence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261103@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261226T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261226T011200
SUMMARY:The obvious can be strange
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nThe most familiar fact may be hardest to se
 e precisely because it has ceased to surprise us.\n\nChesterton uses parad
 ox to make the overfamiliar strange enough to see again.\n\nSource: essays
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261104@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261227T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261227T011200
SUMMARY:A monastery can be worldly
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nWithdrawal from ordinary society does not guar
 antee freedom from ambition\, status\, or self-deception.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Less can enable more”): One asks what enter
 s awareness\; the other asks which environment trains that awareness. The 
 connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nWith John Cage
  (“Silence is not empty”): Merton and Cage both find that silence is n
 ot blank. For Merton it can strip away performed identity\; for Cage it re
 veals the sound-world already happening.\n\nSource: journals and monastic 
 writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261105@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261228T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261228T011200
SUMMARY:Individuation is relation
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nBecoming oneself is not withdrawal into private un
 iqueness\; it requires a changed relation to both unconscious material and
  other people.\n\nSource: individuation writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261106@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261229T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261229T011200
SUMMARY:Art knows by making
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nArtistic intelligence can know its object t
 hrough disciplined making rather than detached inspection alone.\n\nMarita
 in insists that artistic truth has its own mode. A poem need not become a 
 syllogism in order to know something.\n\nSource: Creative Intuition in Art
  and Poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261107@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261230T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261230T011200
SUMMARY:Anxiety reveals freedom
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nAnxiety can disclose possibility itself: 
 the unsettling fact that one can become otherwise.\n\nKierkegaard treats t
 he self as a task\, not a possession. Choice matters because serious choic
 es participate in forming the chooser.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Carl Jung (
 “Projection hides ownership”): Freedom is not external to identity\; r
 epeated choices participate in building the self that will make the next c
 hoice.\n\nSource: The Concept of Anxiety.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261108@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261231T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261231T011200
SUMMARY:Professional need is sticky
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nOnce a need is professionally defined\, institut
 ions gain an incentive to reproduce the category of deficiency they exist 
 to serve.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (“A monastery can be world
 ly”): One asks what enters awareness\; the other asks which environment 
 trains that awareness. The connection is ecological: perception has infras
 tructure.\n\nSource: social criticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261109@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270101T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270101T011200
SUMMARY:What owns attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nA recurring thought can organize perceptio
 n and action before argument ever begins.\n\nDistinction: A thought and th
 e truth of what the thought says are different questions. Evagrius often b
 egins with the first.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Carl Jung (“Individuation is
  relation”): Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong t
 o different psychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a
  degree of autonomy rather than transparent expressions of consciousness.\
 n\nWith Thomas Merton (“A monastery can be worldly”): Both thoughts al
 ter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks what 
 becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perceptio
 n in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic psychology and 
 the analysis of recurring thoughts\; Thomas Merton's is contemplation\, th
 e false self\, and social presence.\n\nSource: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261110@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270102T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270102T011200
SUMMARY:Restoration widens hope
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nJudgment and transformation can be imagined within a 
 cosmic horizon larger than immediate retribution.\n\nSource: speculative t
 heology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261111@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270103T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270103T011200
SUMMARY:The knower has limits
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nLearned ignorance is not anti-intellectual.
  It is intelligence becoming exact about the limits of its own instrument.
 \n\nCusa uses precision to discover the limits of precision. Better measur
 ement can increase knowledge and clarify what measurement cannot reach.\n\
 nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (“A monastery can be worldly”): Read
  today's thought backward through Thomas Merton: “A monastery can be wor
 ldly.” Both define freedom structurally\, in relation to limits\, habits
 \, rules\, control\, or the capacity to answer.\n\nSource: On Learned Igno
 rance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261112@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270104T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270104T011200
SUMMARY:A character can escape
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performed persona can acquire enough continui
 ty that the distinction between actor\, character\, and public event becom
 es unstable.\n\nObservation: Kaufman makes interpretation participatory: t
 he spectator has to risk being wrong about what kind of event this is.\n\n
 CONVERSATION\nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Anxiety reveals freedom”): Kau
 fman destabilizes certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes cer
 tainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for d
 eciding where to stand.\n\nSource: Tony Clifton performances.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261113@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270105T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270105T011200
SUMMARY:Perfection can move
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nPerfection need not mean static completion\
 ; it can be endless growth into an inexhaustible good.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWi
 th Nicholas of Cusa (“The knower has limits”): Gregory's endless ascen
 t and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress compatible 
 with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.\n\nSource: epektasis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261114@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270106T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270106T011200
SUMMARY:Reputation is poor armor
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA spiritual reputation can become another identit
 y to defend\, making criticism more dangerous to the ego than failure itse
 lf.\n\nAbba Moses often teaches by concrete gesture rather than abstract m
 axim. The comic image does philosophical work.\n\nSource: sayings traditio
 n.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261115@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270107T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270107T011200
SUMMARY:The future can interrupt
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nThe future is not only what arrives later. 
 Anticipated possibility can break the spell of what currently looks necess
 ary.\n\nMoltmann refuses consolation that closes history too soon. Sufferi
 ng stays real\, but it is denied the right to become the final metaphysica
 l fact.\n\nSource: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261116@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270108T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270108T011200
SUMMARY:Loneliness is not solitude
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nPhysical aloneness can either intensify fr
 agmentation or become a disciplined form of presence\; the difference is n
 ot automatic.\n\nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261117@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270109T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270109T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can be unsettling
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nSensuous beauty can carry melancholy\
 , fixation\, and danger rather than functioning as uncomplicated consolati
 on.\n\nProvocation: Beauty is not necessarily consoling. Rossetti often ma
 kes it the surface through which fixation and mortality become harder to i
 gnore.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Nicholas of Cusa (“The knower has limits”
 ): An image can hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a w
 ay of remaining precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: poetry and pai
 nting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261118@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270110T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270110T011200
SUMMARY:Learn your ignorance
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nRecognizing the structure of one's ignoranc
 e can be a positive form of knowledge rather than mere deficiency.\n\nCusa
  uses precision to discover the limits of precision. Better measurement ca
 n increase knowledge and clarify what measurement cannot reach.\n\nCONVERS
 ATION\nWith Gregory of Nyssa (“Perfection can move”): Gregory's endles
 s ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual progress comp
 atible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.\n\nSource: On Learne
 d Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261119@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270111T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270111T011200
SUMMARY:Will creates a center
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nWill gathers diffuse possibility into a center 
 of action\, creating both agency and the possibility of self-enclosure.\n\
 nImage: Desire contracts like a whirlpool\, gathering a center while riski
 ng enclosure in the center it creates.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261120@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270112T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270112T011200
SUMMARY:Humility is accurate sight
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nHumility is not self-hatred but increasingl
 y accurate perception of oneself in relation to reality.\n\nSource: Interi
 or Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261121@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270113T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270113T011200
SUMMARY:Resurrection is protest
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nResurrection means that death is real witho
 ut being granted the authority to define the whole.\n\nMoltmann's hope is 
 not positive thinking. It is a protest against allowing the present to def
 ine the limits of the possible.\n\nSource: Theology of Hope\; The Crucifie
 d God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261122@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270114T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270114T011200
SUMMARY:Scarcity can be produced
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nInstitutions can manufacture dependence by redef
 ining ordinary capacities as deficiencies requiring professional supply.\n
 \nIllich's signature question is threshold: when does a tool that extends 
 human capacity begin producing dependence instead?\n\nSource: social criti
 cism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261123@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270115T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270115T011200
SUMMARY:A poem can enact
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA poem can perform the movement it describes\
 , making form part of meaning.\n\nHerbert's forms do not decorate the thou
 ght\; they participate in thinking it.\n\nImage: An altar\, a pulley\, a c
 ollar: Herbert keeps turning ordinary objects into thinking devices.\n\nSo
 urce: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261124@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270116T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270116T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can keep growing
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nFulfillment need not extinguish desire. Enc
 ounter can enlarge the capacity for further encounter.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWi
 th George Herbert (“A poem can enact”): An image can hold what a conce
 pt cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of remaining precise without f
 orcing closure.\n\nWith Nicholas of Cusa (“Learn your ignorance”): Gre
 gory's endless ascent and Cusa's learned ignorance both make intellectual 
 progress compatible with increasing awareness of inexhaustibility.\n\nSour
 ce: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261125@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270117T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270117T011200
SUMMARY:Desire needs empty space
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nAn overfilled interior life leaves little 
 room for anything not already anticipated by existing preference.\n\nCONVE
 RSATION\nWith Gregory of Nyssa (“Desire can keep growing”): Gregory's 
 darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equ
 ation of obscurity with absence.\n\nWith George Herbert (“A poem can ena
 ct”): An image can hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination become
 s a way of remaining precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: Ascent of
  Mount Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261126@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270118T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270118T011200
SUMMARY:Desire colors the scene
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nAn image can reveal as much about the
  structure of longing as about the object apparently represented.\n\nRosse
 tti often lets image and poem answer one another rather than assigning one
  to illustrate the other.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Gregory of Nyssa (“Desir
 e can keep growing”): Desire changes when its object cannot be conceptua
 lly possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more exact.\n\nSo
 urce: poetry and painting.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261127@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270119T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270119T011200
SUMMARY:Love reaches farther
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDesire and love may reach toward what analy
 tic comprehension cannot contain.\n\nThe Cloud author treats conceptual ab
 undance as a possible obstacle. More thoughts can mean less contact.\n\nCO
 NVERSATION\nWith Dante Gabriel Rossetti (“Desire colors the scene”): B
 oth thoughts treat desire as educable. Wanting is not a fixed appetite but
  something altered by waiting\, loss\, attention\, and the object it appro
 aches. The Cloud author's route is apophatic contemplation and the discipl
 ine of unknowing\; Dante Gabriel Rossetti's is Pre-Raphaelite image\, poet
 ry\, desire\, and revision.\n\nWith John of the Cross (“Desire needs emp
 ty space”): Desire changes when its object cannot be conceptually posses
 sed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more exact.\n\nSource: The 
 Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261128@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270120T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270120T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination is a faculty
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nImagination is not merely fantasy opposed to r
 eality\; it is a mode through which reality becomes humanly apprehensible.
 \n\nHis contraries are generative: energy appears through tension\, not af
 ter every opposition has been domesticated.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Clou
 d author (“Love reaches farther”): Desire colors the image\, while the
  image educates desire. Each can quietly teach the other what to want.\n\n
 Source: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261129@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T011200
SUMMARY:Shape changes reading
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nVisual arrangement can alter tempo\, emphasis
 \, and conceptual relation before the words are paraphrased.\n\nHis devoti
 onal poetry is unusually willing to argue\, complain\, pun\, reverse itsel
 f\, and make those motions part of prayer.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of t
 he Cross (“Desire needs empty space”): Language reaches its limit with
 out becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must giv
 e way to unsaying.\n\nSource: pattern poems.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261130@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270122T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270122T011200
SUMMARY:Tools reshape users
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nA tool does not merely extend an ability. R
 epeated use can reorganize the sensorium and the user who uses it.\n\nMcLu
 han repeatedly shifts the question from 'what are we using?' to 'what kind
  of users are being produced by repeated use?'\n\nSource: Understanding Me
 dia.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261201@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270123T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270123T011200
SUMMARY:Prudence is practical truth
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nPractical wisdom concerns truth in action: 
 not merely knowing a rule\, but judging what this situation asks.\n\nObser
 vation: Maritain gives form its own demands. Sincerity matters\, but since
 rity does not finish the work.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith George Herbert (“Sh
 ape changes reading”): Maritain's claim that art knows through making be
 comes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work parap
 hrase cannot replace.\n\nSource: moral philosophy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261202@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270124T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270124T011200
SUMMARY:A symbol exceeds a sign
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA living symbol does not merely encode a known mea
 ning. It gathers meanings that cannot yet be stated exhaustively.\n\nJung'
 s psyche is plural and only partly transparent to consciousness. Integrati
 on is therefore not the same thing as suppression or neat consistency.\n\n
 Distinction: A symbol is not a code with a hidden answer key. A living sym
 bol holds more meaning than consciousness can presently cash out.\n\nCONVE
 RSATION\nWith John of the Cross (“Desire needs empty space”): Language
  reaches its limit without becoming useless. The interesting point is wher
 e saying truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: symbols and transform
 ation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261203@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270125T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270125T011200
SUMMARY:Repair the larger whole
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA good local change improves not only 
 its target but the larger field of relationships around it.\n\nAlexander's
  language of 'life' is not just aesthetic mood. He is making a structural 
 claim about how centers reinforce one another and how local changes affect
  a larger field.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jacques Maritain (“Prudence is pr
 actical truth”): Maritain's sense that form can be intrinsically intelli
 gible meets Alexander's insistence that good form is relational and alive\
 , not merely imposed from outside.\n\nSource: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261204@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270126T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270126T011200
SUMMARY:Spaceship Earth has no edge
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nThinking of Earth as a shared vehicle cha
 nges the intuition that waste can simply be thrown 'away.'\n\nObservation:
  Fuller's favorite scale shift moves quickly from the object in hand to th
 e planetary system that supplies\, uses\, and discards it.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Christopher Alexander (“Repair the larger whole”): Alexander an
 d Fuller both think relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller 
 abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether th
 e resulting form actually feels more alive.\n\nSource: Operating Manual fo
 r Spaceship Earth.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261205@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T011200
SUMMARY:Help can disable
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nProfessionalized help can sometimes erode the ca
 pacities and relationships it intends to support.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Bu
 ckminster Fuller (“Spaceship Earth has no edge”): Fuller trusts redesi
 gn to unlock abundance\; Illich worries that systems can become counterpro
 ductive even when technically successful. Their disagreement is a useful b
 rake on either thinker alone.\n\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“Tools reshape u
 sers”): McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes its user\; Illich asks when a
  tool crosses the threshold from extending human capacity to disabling it.
  Together they enlarge the question 'what does this tool do?'\n\nSource: D
 eschooling Society\; Medical Nemesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261206@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270128T011200
SUMMARY:Poverty can be inward
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nInner poverty can mean freedom even from the
  demand to possess one's own spiritual state.\n\nEckhart is suspicious eve
 n of spiritual possessions. A true idea can become false in use when the s
 elf clutches it as property.\n\nSource: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261207@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270129T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270129T011200
SUMMARY:Content is the decoy
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nThe obvious content of a medium can distrac
 t from the structural changes the medium itself produces.\n\nMcLuhan is le
 ss interested in what a medium says than in the unnoticed environment it c
 reates around saying.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Ivan Illich (“Help can disab
 le”): McLuhan asks how a medium remakes its users\; Illich asks whether 
 that remaking serves or monopolizes human competence. They are diagnosing 
 two sides of the same technological threshold.\n\nSource: Understanding Me
 dia.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261208@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270130T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270130T011200
SUMMARY:Nothingness can free
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nBecoming 'nothing' names a release from self
 -importance and possession\, not the destruction of personhood.\n\nCONVERS
 ATION\nWith Carl Jung (“A symbol exceeds a sign”): Language reaches it
 s limit without becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying tr
 uly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: sermons on poverty.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261209@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270131T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270131T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can be mercy
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nSilence can sometimes protect another perso
 n from the violence of one's need to correct\, classify\, or prevail.\n\nC
 ONVERSATION\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“Content is the decoy”): One asks 
 what enters awareness\; the other asks which environment trains that aware
 ness. The connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSour
 ce: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261210@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270201T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270201T011200
SUMMARY:Freedom survives providence
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nDivine providence need not erase creaturely freedom\;
  moral development requires real participation.\n\nProvocation: A reading 
 that leaves the reader entirely unchanged may have extracted information w
 hile missing the kind of truth Origen seeks.\n\nSource: On First Principle
 s.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261211@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270202T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270202T011200
SUMMARY:The medium acts first
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nA medium changes habits\, scale\, pace\, an
 d relation before its explicit content is considered.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWit
 h Ivan Illich (“Help can disable”): McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes
  its user\; Illich asks when a tool crosses the threshold from extending h
 uman capacity to disabling it. Together they enlarge the question 'what do
 es this tool do?'\n\nQUOTATION\n“The medium is the message.” — Marsh
 all McLuhan\n\nSource: Understanding Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261212@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270203T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270203T011200
SUMMARY:Courage belongs inward
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nInterior work can require practical courage
  because it unsettles identities and habits that once organized the self.\
 n\nTeresa's interior castle is a map of differentiated inwardness\, not a 
 claim that the self is simple or immediately knowable.\n\nSource: Interior
  Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261213@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270204T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270204T011200
SUMMARY:Irony creates distance
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nIrony can loosen the grip of an identity 
 or social script by creating distance from what previously seemed self-evi
 dent.\n\nKierkegaard treats the self as a task\, not a possession. Choice 
 matters because serious choices participate in forming the chooser.\n\nSou
 rce: The Concept of Irony.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261214@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270205T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270205T011200
SUMMARY:Fairy tales teach limits
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nFairy tales make arbitrary limits vivid\, a
 nd thereby dramatize the fact that freedom always takes shape within condi
 tions.\n\nHis defense of limits is playful rather than cramped: boundaries
  create the field in which a form or game becomes possible.\n\nSource: Ort
 hodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261215@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270206T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270206T011200
SUMMARY:Contraries make progress
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nCreative movement may require contraries rathe
 r than the premature elimination of tension.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Søren 
 Kierkegaard (“Irony creates distance”): Freedom is not external to ide
 ntity\; repeated choices participate in building the self that will make t
 he next choice.\n\nWith G. K. Chesterton (“Fairy tales teach limits”):
  Blake and Chesterton use paradox to dislodge the anesthetic of habit. Bla
 ke's result is visionary revolt\; Chesterton's\, renewed astonishment at e
 xistence.\n\nQUOTATION\n“Without contraries is no progression.” — Wi
 lliam Blake\, The Marriage of Heaven and Hell\n\nSource: The Marriage of H
 eaven and Hell.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261216@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270207T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270207T011200
SUMMARY:Every name is provisional
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nEven the best theological name must finally
  be denied if it begins to function as an adequate definition.\n\nFor Dion
 ysius\, excess can look like darkness: the instrument fails not because no
 thing is present\, but because what is present exceeds its capacity.\n\nSo
 urce: Divine Names\; Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261217@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270208T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270208T011200
SUMMARY:Enter the unknowing
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDiscursive thought can reach a point where 
 further conceptual accumulation obstructs contemplation.\n\nA short word o
 r simple desire can concentrate attention more effectively than an elabora
 te spiritual explanation.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Pseudo-Dionysius (“Every
  name is provisional”): Read today's thought backward through Pseudo-Dio
 nysius: “Every name is provisional.” Both change what can be perceived
  by subtracting something: one loosens selective attention\, the other loo
 sens conceptual certainty.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261218@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270209T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270209T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nNegation can free thought from mistaking it
 s concepts for the reality those concepts attempt to name.\n\nDionysius tr
 eats theological language as both necessary and inadequate. The name can d
 isclose\, then must be surrendered before it hardens into possession.\n\nC
 ONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Enter the unknowing”): The Cloud 
 author makes a practice of the unknowing Dionysius describes: conceptual s
 ubtraction becomes something enacted by attention and desire.\n\nSource: M
 ystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261219@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T011200
SUMMARY:Play changes the player
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nThe deepest games are not simply activities o
 ne performs\; participation alters the identity from which one plays.\n\nC
 arse's language sounds light\, but it is severe about identity: titles\, v
 ictories\, and roles become dangerous when yesterday's win decides what to
 morrow is allowed to be.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261220@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270211T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270211T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can be redirected
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nPassion need not simply be annihilated
 \; desire can be transformed by changing what and how it loves.\n\nObserva
 tion: For Maximus\, ascetic practice is epistemic: desire reorganized by l
 ove begins to see a different world.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud autho
 r (“Enter the unknowing”): Desire changes when its object cannot be co
 nceptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more exact
 .\n\nSource: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261221@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270212T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270212T011200
SUMMARY:Appearance is an event
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nAppearance need not be a veil hiding reality. App
 earing is itself one of the ways reality happens.\n\nFink's great existent
 ial phenomena—play\, love\, death\, work\, domination—are ways the wor
 ld becomes intelligible through lived structures.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Ja
 mes P. Carse (“Play changes the player”): Carse and Fink take play ser
 iously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes conti
 nuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itsel
 f.\n\nSource: phenomenological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261222@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270213T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270213T011200
SUMMARY:Friendship can be method
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nPrayer can be understood less as technique 
 than as sustained friendship\, changing its logic from performance to rela
 tion.\n\nSource: Life.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261223@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270214T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270214T011200
SUMMARY:The future needs players
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nAn infinite game has no point without future 
 participants. Its ethics therefore include preserving conditions for peopl
 e not yet present.\n\nCarse treats rules as tools of a game rather than sa
 cred furniture. In infinite play\, even the rules remain answerable to the
  continuation of play.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Eugen Fink (“Appearance is 
 an event”): Fink shows how play creates a world\; Carse asks how to prev
 ent a game from closing the world it created. World-making and world-keepi
 ng are different tasks.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261224@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270215T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270215T011200
SUMMARY:Boredom can be active
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nWithholding conventional payoff can make an aud
 ience newly aware of its own impatience\, entitlement\, and interpretive l
 abor.\n\nKaufman's material is often the audience's certainty about what k
 ind of event is occurring.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Pseudo-Dionysius (“Remo
 ve one certainty”): Kaufman's refusal to clarify the performance frame h
 as an odd kinship with apophatic refusal: certainty is withheld\, though f
 or radically different ends.\n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261225@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270216T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270216T011200
SUMMARY:Innocence is not ignorance
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nInnocence and experience are states of percept
 ion\, not simply chronological stages one leaves behind forever.\n\nSource
 : Songs of Innocence and of Experience.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261226@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270217T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270217T011200
SUMMARY:Illness changes practice
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nAscetic practice must respond to ac
 tual bodies rather than to abstract ideals of toughness.\n\nHer sayings ke
 ep bodily reality in view. Practice must be truthful about illness\, capac
 ity\, and circumstance.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William Blake (“Innocence 
 is not ignorance”): ” Attention is being trained as a practice rather 
 than treated as a neutral faculty one simply possesses.\n\nWith Eugen Fink
  (“Appearance is an event”): Practice turns identity from a descriptio
 n into an experiment. Repetition reveals what the self can actually sustai
 n.\n\nSource: sayings attributed to Syncletica.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261227@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270218T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270218T011200
SUMMARY:Energy resists cages
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nVitality can be distorted when moral systems t
 reat energy itself as the enemy rather than asking what form it takes.\n\n
 Blake distrusts any system that becomes a substitute for vision. The dange
 r is not thought but thought hardened into a cage for perception.\n\nSourc
 e: The Marriage of Heaven and Hell.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261228@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270219T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270219T011200
SUMMARY:Refusal can be creative
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nSaying no can be a positive act of form-m
 aking when refusal protects another commitment.\n\nHer restraint is formal
 ly active: what is withheld often concentrates the force of what remains.\
 n\nSource: devotional poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261229@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270220T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270220T011200
SUMMARY:Goodness may look strange
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nMoral transformation can initially feel ali
 en because it conflicts with the self's established picture of what fulfil
 lment should be.\n\nHis fairy tales resist the demand to cash every image 
 into a moral. The image is allowed to keep working after explanation has s
 topped.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William Blake (“Innocence is not ignorance
 ”): MacDonald and Blake treat imagination as a means of apprehending rea
 lity\, not an ornamental escape from it. Their imaginative worlds expose t
 he poverty of a merely literal account.\n\nSource: novels and fairy tales.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261230@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270221T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270221T011200
SUMMARY:Scripture has depths
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA text may yield different levels of meaning as the r
 eader and the question change.\n\nImage: The literal text is a doorway\, n
 ot a disposable shell: one enters through it\, but need not remain standin
 g in the threshold.\n\nSource: On First Principles and homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20261231@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270222T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270222T011200
SUMMARY:Maps can change decisions
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nA good map can make planetary interdepend
 ence perceptible enough to alter practical judgment.\n\nFuller habitually 
 moves from object to system: what matters is often the invisible distribut
 ion of forces\, resources\, and relations.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William B
 lake (“Innocence is not ignorance”): One asks what enters awareness\; 
 the other asks which environment trains that awareness. The connection is 
 ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSource: Dymaxion map.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270101@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270223T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270223T011200
SUMMARY:Stay with the struggle
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nLeaving a difficult situation can sometime
 s preserve the very pattern one hoped to escape.\n\nProvocation: The urge 
 to leave may be wisdom. It may also be the pattern's favorite survival str
 ategy.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Syncletica of Alexandria (“Illness changes 
 practice”): Anthony and Syncletica distrust geographical solutions to in
 terior problems. The desert does not magically remove what the traveler br
 ings.\n\nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270102@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270224T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270224T011200
SUMMARY:Limits can free action
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nChosen limits can protect competence\, convivial
 ity\, and local agency that unlimited systems tend to absorb.\n\nCONVERSAT
 ION\nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Maps can change decisions”): Fuller tru
 sts redesign to unlock abundance\; Illich worries that systems can become 
 counterproductive even when technically successful. Their disagreement is 
 a useful brake on either thinker alone.\n\nSource: Tools for Conviviality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270103@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270225T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270225T011200
SUMMARY:Grace disrupts
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nTransformation may first appear as inter
 ruption\, humiliation\, violence to self-image\, or loss of control.\n\nGr
 ace in O'Connor is rarely niceness. It often arrives as an assault on the 
 character's preferred self-description.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster 
 Fuller (“Maps can change decisions”): One asks what enters awareness\;
  the other asks which environment trains that awareness. The connection is
  ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nQUOTATION\n“The truth doe
 s not change according to our ability to stomach it.” — Flannery O’C
 onnor\n\nSource: fiction and essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270104@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270226T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270226T011200
SUMMARY:Mystery resists slogans
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nA story can embody mystery without reduc
 ing it to a detachable moral proposition.\n\nO'Connor keeps the comic and 
 the terrible close together. Laughter can expose self-deception more effic
 iently than solemn moral instruction.\n\nSource: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270105@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270227T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270227T011200
SUMMARY:The world is no object
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nThe world is not one more thing among things. It 
 is the horizon within which things can appear at all.\n\nFink treats play 
 cosmologically rather than recreationally: play discloses something about 
 world-making itself.\n\nSource: phenomenological writings on world.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270106@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270228T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270228T011200
SUMMARY:Humility can be concrete
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nHumility becomes credible when enacted in situati
 ons where status\, injury\, and recognition are actually at stake.\n\nAbba
  Moses often teaches by concrete gesture rather than abstract maxim. The c
 omic image does philosophical work.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Anthony the Grea
 t (“Stay with the struggle”): Practice turns identity from a descripti
 on into an experiment. Repetition reveals what the self can actually susta
 in.\n\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“Grace disrupts”): O'Connor and Abba 
 Moses distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction\, the other c
 omic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see everyone else's fa
 ult.\n\nSource: sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270107@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270301T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270301T011200
SUMMARY:The infinite breaks scale
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nFinite measures cannot simply be extrapolat
 ed until they become adequate to the infinite.\n\nHis paradoxes are not ri
 ddles for their own sake. They are instruments for exposing where finite c
 ategories stop scaling.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Maps 
 can change decisions”): Cusa's geometrical metaphors and Fuller's struct
 ural thinking share a fascination with how abstract form reorganizes intui
 tion\, though one aims at learned ignorance and the other at design.\n\nWi
 th Eugen Fink (“The world is no object”): Both resist the isolated uni
 t. Form emerges from relations\, while relation becomes perceptible throug
 h form.\n\nSource: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270108@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270302T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270302T011200
SUMMARY:Do not flee too quickly
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nChanging places does not necessaril
 y change the passions carried from place to place.\n\nHer sayings keep bod
 ily reality in view. Practice must be truthful about illness\, capacity\, 
 and circumstance.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Abba Moses (“Humility can be con
 crete”): Practice turns identity from a description into an experiment. 
 Repetition reveals what the self can actually sustain.\n\nWith Anthony the
  Great (“Stay with the struggle”): Anthony and Syncletica distrust geo
 graphical solutions to interior problems. The desert does not magically re
 move what the traveler brings.\n\nSource: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270109@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270303T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270303T011200
SUMMARY:The ground is deeper
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nThe deepest ground of the self may not coinc
 ide with the familiar ego that narrates itself.\n\nEckhart is suspicious e
 ven of spiritual possessions. A true idea can become false in use when the
  self clutches it as property.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (
 “Grace disrupts”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The
  useful friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other
  asks what had been filtering perception in the first place. Meister Eckha
 rt's route is detachment\, inward poverty\, and apophatic preaching\; Flan
 nery O’Connor's is grotesque fiction and a Catholic theology of grace.\n
 \nSource: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270110@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270304T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270304T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nPossession of an idea can obstruct encounter
  when the idea becomes a substitute for what it names.\n\nEckhart pushes i
 nward until the familiar narrated self is no longer the deepest ground of 
 identity.\n\nSource: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270111@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270305T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270305T011200
SUMMARY:Love can contain paradox
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nContradictions that seem irresolvable at o
 ne level can be held within a larger claim about love without being cheapl
 y explained away.\n\nJulian's confidence does not come from denying wounds
 . Her distinctive move is to deny them the final interpretive authority.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“Mystery resists slogans”): 
 Desire colors the image\, while the image educates desire. Each can quietl
 y teach the other what to want.\n\nSource: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270112@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270306T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270306T011200
SUMMARY:Gratitude begins in excess
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nGratitude can arise from perceiving existen
 ce as gift before calculating whether circumstances meet expectations.\n\n
 Image: Fairy-tale logic says the apple may be enchanted precisely because 
 apples exist at all.\n\nSource: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270113@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270307T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270307T011200
SUMMARY:Love outruns calculation
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nLove becomes recognizably merciful when it 
 gives up the fantasy that every good must be proportioned to deserving.\n\
 nIsaac pushes compassion past ordinary moral arithmetic. The interesting t
 hreshold is where mercy stops being proportional to deserving.\n\nCONVERSA
 TION\nWith Julian of Norwich (“Love can contain paradox”): Julian and 
 Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy without making suffering imagi
 nary. Both press compassion beyond ordinary punitive accounting.\n\nWith A
 bba Moses (“Humility can be concrete”): Moses makes judgment ridiculou
 s by showing the judge's own failures leaking behind him. Isaac goes furth
 er by replacing accounting with expansive mercy.\n\nSource: Ascetical Homi
 lies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270114@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270308T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270308T011200
SUMMARY:Medicine can create illness
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nA system devoted to health can create new forms 
 of harm when intervention\, dependency\, and medicalization exceed useful 
 thresholds.\n\nIllich's signature question is threshold: when does a tool 
 that extends human capacity begin producing dependence instead?\n\nCONVERS
 ATION\nWith Meister Eckhart (“The ground is deeper”): One asks what en
 ters awareness\; the other asks which environment trains that awareness. T
 he connection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSource: Med
 ical Nemesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270115@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270309T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270309T011200
SUMMARY:Literal sense is a door
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA literal reading can be genuine without exhausting t
 he possible meanings of a text.\n\nDistinction: Multiple senses do not mea
 n anything goes. Origen's richer readings remain ordered toward transforma
 tion and coherence.\n\nSource: biblical exegesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270116@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270310T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270310T011200
SUMMARY:Early fervor can mislead
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nEnthusiasm at the beginning of a pr
 actice can conceal whether it has become durable enough to survive boredom
  and difficulty.\n\nShe distrusts geographical solutions to interior probl
 ems: the passion usually travels with the person who is fleeing.\n\nSource
 : sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270117@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270311T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270311T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty is not prettiness
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nBeauty concerns a radiance of form apprehen
 ded in things\; it is not reducible to charm\, polish\, or agreeable subje
 ct matter.\n\nMaritain is a realist about art: poetic intuition is not pri
 vate whim but a mode of contact with being through making.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Julian of Norwich (“Love can contain paradox”): Desire colors t
 he image\, while the image educates desire. Each can quietly teach the oth
 er what to want.\n\nSource: Art and Scholasticism.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270118@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270312T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270312T011200
SUMMARY:A difficulty may be placed
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA textual difficulty can provoke deeper reading rathe
 r than simply signal failure.\n\nObservation: Origen's speculative boldnes
 s comes partly from taking divine pedagogy seriously across a scale larger
  than immediate reward or punishment.\n\nSource: On First Principles.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270119@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270313T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270313T011200
SUMMARY:A part can mediate
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nA local relation can participate in re
 conciling larger divisions rather than remaining merely local.\n\nFor Maxi
 mus\, love is epistemic as well as moral: distorted relation can become di
 storted perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“Love outrun
 s calculation”): Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion m
 eet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private 
 self.\n\nSource: Ambigua.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270120@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270314T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270314T011200
SUMMARY:Old media become art
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWhen a medium is displaced\, features once 
 taken for granted can become newly visible and aestheticized.\n\nMcLuhan r
 epeatedly shifts the question from 'what are we using?' to 'what kind of u
 sers are being produced by repeated use?'\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Ivan Illic
 h (“Medicine can create illness”): McLuhan asks how a medium reshapes 
 its user\; Illich asks when a tool crosses the threshold from extending hu
 man capacity to disabling it. Together they enlarge the question 'what doe
 s this tool do?'\n\nSource: Laws of Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270121@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270315T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270315T011200
SUMMARY:Force turns persons to things
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nViolence can reduce a person\, socially and imag
 inatively\, toward the condition of an object.\n\nHer thought is severe ab
 out necessity: one must see constraint exactly without confusing what is n
 ecessary with what is good.\n\nSource: The Iliad\, or the Poem of Force.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270122@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270316T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270316T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can arrive late
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nAn experience may remain partially unintel
 ligible for years before a later insight reorganizes it.\n\nJulian can hol
 d unresolved contradiction without rushing to explain it away. Hope is all
 owed to be firmer than the available theory.\n\nSource: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270123@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270317T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270317T011200
SUMMARY:Faith risks the absurd
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nFaith is not the elimination of uncertain
 ty but a mode of commitment that cannot be secured by objective guarantees
 .\n\nKierkegaard treats the self as a task\, not a possession. Choice matt
 ers because serious choices participate in forming the chooser.\n\nSource:
  Fear and Trembling.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270124@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270318T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270318T011200
SUMMARY:Sanity can hold extremes
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA sane synthesis may preserve two strong tr
 uths in tension rather than weakening each until conflict disappears.\n\nO
 bservation: Chesterton's paradoxes are perspective machines: they rotate a
 n assumption until its hidden strangeness comes into view.\n\nWonder\, for
  Chesterton\, begins when existence stops being treated as the default set
 ting of the universe.\n\nSource: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270125@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270319T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270319T011200
SUMMARY:Letting go is active
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nDetachment is not indifference but a discipl
 ined openness that refuses to clutch at outcomes\, identities\, and consol
 ations.\n\nEckhart is suspicious even of spiritual possessions. A true ide
 a can become false in use when the self clutches it as property.\n\nSource
 : sermons and treatises.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270126@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270320T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270320T011200
SUMMARY:Tenderness can be strong
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nMercy need not be sentimentality\; it can d
 emand a more difficult refusal to reduce a person to an offense.\n\nCONVER
 SATION\nWith Simone Weil (“Force turns persons to things”): Weil and I
 saac stretch attention beyond the self's preferred circle. Weil emphasizes
  the reality of the afflicted other\; Isaac lets compassion widen toward a
 ll creation.\n\nWith Julian of Norwich (“Meaning can arrive late”): Ju
 lian and Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy without making suffer
 ing imaginary. Both press compassion beyond ordinary punitive accounting.\
 n\nSource: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270127@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270321T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270321T011200
SUMMARY:Precision always escapes
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nFinite measurement can improve indefinitely
  without becoming identical to perfect precision.\n\nLearned ignorance is 
 intellectual achievement\, not surrender: the knower becomes more exact ab
 out the limits of the knowing instrument.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith G. K. Ches
 terton (“Sanity can hold extremes”): Language reaches its limit withou
 t becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give 
 way to unsaying.\n\nWith Meister Eckhart (“Letting go is active”): An 
 image can hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of 
 remaining precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: On Learned Ignorance
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270128@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270322T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270322T011200
SUMMARY:Rules can serve change
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nRules need not merely constrain play. In an o
 pen game they can be altered to preserve the possibility of continuing.\n\
 nCarse treats rules as tools of a game rather than sacred furniture. In in
 finite play\, even the rules remain answerable to the continuation of play
 .\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270129@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270323T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270323T011200
SUMMARY:Concepts can become clouds
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nThought can obscure as well as illuminate w
 hen it multiplies representations faster than attention deepens.\n\nUnknow
 ing here is disciplined rather than vague: the mind relinquishes the deman
 d to convert encounter immediately into possession.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith 
 Nicholas of Cusa (“Precision always escapes”): Both thoughts make inte
 llectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The questi
 on is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed
  desire. The Cloud author's route is apophatic contemplation and the disci
 pline of unknowing\; Nicholas of Cusa's is learned ignorance\, infinity\, 
 and mathematical metaphor.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270130@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270324T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270324T011200
SUMMARY:Where is the frame?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nWhen an audience cannot locate the boundary bet
 ween performance and event\, its expectations become part of the work.\n\n
 Kaufman's material is often the audience's certainty about what kind of ev
 ent is occurring.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“Rules can serve
  change”): Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the stronge
 st performance often leaves the audience without a final verdict about wha
 t game was being played.\n\nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Faith risks the ab
 surd”): Kaufman destabilizes certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard des
 tabilizes certainty about borrowed conviction. Both make the observer resp
 onsible for deciding where to stand.\n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270131@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270325T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270325T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination is serious
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nImagination can enlarge the field in which 
 usefulness itself is later judged.\n\nHis fairy tales resist the demand to
  cash every image into a moral. The image is allowed to keep working after
  explanation has stopped.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith G. K. Chesterton (“Sanit
 y can hold extremes”): Chesterton inherits much from MacDonald's imagina
 tive Christianity: wonder is not childish simplification but a discipline 
 for seeing the ordinary as contingent and strange.\n\nSource: The Fantasti
 c Imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270201@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270326T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270326T011200
SUMMARY:We inhabit more than tasks
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nHuman existence cannot be exhausted by projects a
 nd functions\; world-experience exceeds the inventory of what we are doing
 .\n\nHis 'world' is not the biggest object. It is the horizon in which obj
 ects\, tasks\, and persons can appear at all.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith James 
 P. Carse (“Rules can serve change”): Carse and Fink take play seriousl
 y enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuati
 on\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.\n\
 nSource: existential phenomenology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270202@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270327T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270327T011200
SUMMARY:Distinguish to unite
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nA good distinction need not fragment realit
 y. It can make deeper forms of unity intelligible.\n\nHis distinctions are
  meant to preserve complexity without breaking the world into unrelated co
 mpartments.\n\nSource: Degrees of Knowledge.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270203@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270328T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270328T011200
SUMMARY:Virtue needs friction
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nResistance can expose and strengthe
 n capacities that ease leaves dormant.\n\nShe distrusts geographical solut
 ions to interior problems: the passion usually travels with the person who
  is fleeing.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“Where is the frame?”
 ): Read today's thought backward through Andy Kaufman: “Where is the fra
 me?.” Freedom appears not as absence of rules but as room for unscripted
  response inside a field.\n\nWith Eugen Fink (“We inhabit more than task
 s”): Practice turns identity from a description into an experiment. Repe
 tition reveals what the self can actually sustain.\n\nSource: Sayings trad
 ition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270204@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270329T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270329T011200
SUMMARY:A tool should leave room
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nA convivial tool expands the user's capacity to 
 act without requiring expert control over the whole field.\n\nSource: Tool
 s for Conviviality.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270205@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270330T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270330T011200
SUMMARY:Duration can be material
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nTime is not merely a container for sounds\; durati
 on itself can become one of composition's primary materials.\n\nCONVERSATI
 ON\nWith The Cloud author (“Concepts can become clouds”): Cage's silen
 ce and the Cloud author's unknowing both change attention by removing expe
 cted content. One opens the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possessi
 on.\n\nWith George MacDonald (“Imagination is serious”): Both thoughts
  alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks wh
 at becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering percep
 tion in the first place. John Cage's route is composition and disciplined 
 listening\; George MacDonald's is Christian imagination and fairy-tale poe
 tics.\n\nSource: compositional practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270206@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270331T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270331T011200
SUMMARY:Devotion can be witty
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nIntellectual play and devotional seriousness 
 need not be opposites\; wit can become a mode of exact attention.\n\nProvo
 cation: Devotional seriousness does not require stylistic solemnity. Wit c
 an be a form of exact attention.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Durat
 ion can be material”): Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, 
 yet both make form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening
 \, Herbert through shaped language.\n\nWith Jacques Maritain (“Distingui
 sh to unite”): Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes co
 ncrete in Herbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work paraphrase ca
 nnot replace.\n\nSource: The Temple.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270207@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270401T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270401T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nAt the limits of finite comparison\, an opp
 osition may expose the limits of the measuring concept itself.\n\nSource: 
 On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270208@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270402T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270402T011200
SUMMARY:The object can instruct
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nAn altar\, pulley\, collar\, flower\, or wind
 ow can become a conceptual instrument rather than mere ornament.\n\nImage:
  An altar\, a pulley\, a collar: Herbert keeps turning ordinary objects in
 to thinking devices.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Duration can be m
 aterial”): Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, yet both mak
 e form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening\, Herbert t
 hrough shaped language.\n\nWith Jacques Maritain (“Distinguish to unite
 ”): Maritain's claim that art knows through making becomes concrete in H
 erbert\, where poetic form does intellectual work paraphrase cannot replac
 e.\n\nSource: The Temple.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270209@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270403T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270403T011200
SUMMARY:Practice reveals doctrine
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nWhat a person actually practices can discl
 ose their operative theology more sharply than their explicit claims.\n\nQ
 uestion: What does this difficulty reveal only because you have remained w
 ith it long enough to stop resetting the experiment?\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith
  John Cage (“Duration can be material”): Read today's thought backward
  through John Cage: “Duration can be material.” Attention is being tra
 ined as a practice rather than treated as a neutral faculty one simply pos
 sesses.\n\nWith Syncletica of Alexandria (“Virtue needs friction”): Bo
 th thoughts treat practice as diagnostic. What repeats reveals the structu
 re of desire\, attention\, and freedom more reliably than declared intenti
 on. Macarius of Egypt's route is the heart\, interior transformation\, and
  experiential asceticism\; Syncletica of Alexandria's is desert discernmen
 t\, patience\, and embodied practice.\n\nSource: Macarian tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270210@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270404T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270404T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can constellate
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nEvents can gather subjective meaning around an act
 ivated psychic pattern even when no simple causal story explains their res
 onance.\n\nFor Jung\, a symbol is alive precisely when it exceeds the expl
 anation currently available for it.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Nicholas of Cusa
  (“Opposites can coincide”): Language reaches its limit without becomi
 ng useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give way to u
 nsaying.\n\nSource: synchronicity and complex theory.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270211@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270405T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270405T011200
SUMMARY:Decreate the center
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nThe self's demand to occupy the center of realit
 y can be loosened so that what is not-self becomes more perceptible.\n\nCO
 NVERSATION\nWith George Herbert (“Devotion can be witty”): Both though
 ts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks 
 what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perc
 eption in the first place. Simone Weil's route is attention\, affliction\,
  force\, and decreation\; George Herbert's is metaphysical poetry\, form\,
  wit\, and devotion.\n\nWith John Cage (“Duration can be material”): W
 eil's attention and Cage's listening meet in a disciplined refusal to deci
 de too quickly what matters. Weil makes it ethical\; Cage makes it acousti
 c.\n\nSource: Gravity and Grace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270212@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270406T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270406T011200
SUMMARY:Attention can descend
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nThe work is not necessarily to climb into e
 ver more complex thought\, but to sink beneath the demand to conceptualize
  everything.\n\nThe Cloud author treats conceptual abundance as a possible
  obstacle. More thoughts can mean less contact.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John
  Cage (“Duration can be material”): Cage's silence and the Cloud autho
 r's unknowing both change attention by removing expected content. One open
 s the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possession.\n\nWith George Her
 bert (“Devotion can be witty”): Both thoughts make intellectual limita
 tion productive rather than merely embarrassing. The question is whether t
 he limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed desire. The Cl
 oud author's route is apophatic contemplation and the discipline of unknow
 ing\; George Herbert's is metaphysical poetry\, form\, wit\, and devotion.
 \n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270213@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270407T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270407T011200
SUMMARY:Loss can alter value
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nSomething relinquished can acquire a diff
 erent meaning after it is no longer available for possession.\n\nHer restr
 aint is formally active: what is withheld often concentrates the force of 
 what remains.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Attention can des
 cend”): Language reaches its limit without becoming useless. The interes
 ting point is where saying truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: poe
 try.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270214@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270408T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270408T011200
SUMMARY:The heart has chambers
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInterior life is not a single transparent 
 space. Contradictory forces can inhabit the same person.\n\nSource: Macari
 an Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270215@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270409T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270409T011200
SUMMARY:God is not your concept
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nThe concept of God can become an idol precis
 ely when it feels most intellectually secure.\n\nEckhart pushes inward unt
 il the familiar narrated self is no longer the deepest ground of identity.
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Attention can descend”): Bot
 h thoughts make intellectual limitation productive rather than merely emba
 rrassing. The question is whether the limit invites silence\, better conce
 pts\, or transformed desire. Meister Eckhart's route is detachment\, inwar
 d poverty\, and apophatic preaching\; The Cloud author's is apophatic cont
 emplation and the discipline of unknowing.\n\nSource: sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270216@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270410T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270410T011200
SUMMARY:Impersonality can protect
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nThe deepest value in a person may be obscured wh
 en everything is filtered through status\, personality\, and social recogn
 ition.\n\nObservation: Weil's most demanding attention is empty in a preci
 se sense: it waits without filling the space with the answer it wants.\n\n
 Source: late essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270217@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270411T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270411T011200
SUMMARY:The small can hold all
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA tiny and vulnerable thing can disclose a
  metaphysical horizon vastly larger than its apparent scale.\n\nJulian's c
 onfidence does not come from denying wounds. Her distinctive move is to de
 ny them the final interpretive authority.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Meister Ec
 khart (“God is not your concept”): Desire changes when its object cann
 ot be conceptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it mo
 re exact.\n\nQUOTATION\n“All shall be well\, and all shall be well\, and
  all manner of thing shall be well.” — Julian of Norwich\, Revelations
  of Divine Love\n\nSource: Revelations of Divine Love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270218@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270412T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270412T011200
SUMMARY:The comic can be severe
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nComedy can expose spiritual disproportio
 n more mercilessly than solemnity.\n\nDistinction: Grace is not niceness\,
  and moral clarity is not the same thing as respectable self-possession.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith Simone Weil (“Decreate the center”): Weil's atten
 tion and O'Connor's violence look opposed\, yet both distrust the self-pro
 tective stories by which a person avoids reality.\n\nSource: fiction and e
 ssays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270219@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270413T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270413T011200
SUMMARY:Strength can become gentleness
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nThe capacities once used for domination can be re
 directed rather than merely suppressed.\n\nMoses's own story gives these s
 ayings weight: transformation does not require pretending the earlier self
  never existed.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“The comic ca
 n be severe”): O'Connor and Abba Moses distrust moral superiority. One u
 ses grotesque fiction\, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the pe
 rson who can see everyone else's fault.\n\nSource: desert tradition surrou
 nding Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270220@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270414T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270414T011200
SUMMARY:Choose\, then become
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA choice is not only a selection among op
 tions\; serious choices participate in forming the chooser.\n\nSource: Eit
 her/Or.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270221@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270415T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270415T011200
SUMMARY:Failure can perform
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nAn apparent failure of entertainment can itself
  become the performance by exposing what an audience believes it is owed.\
 n\nKaufman's ambiguity is not simply trickery. By refusing to stabilize th
 e frame\, he turns interpretation into part of the event.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Choose\, then become”): Kaufman destabilize
 s certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes certainty about bor
 rowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding where to
  stand.\n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270222@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270416T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270416T011200
SUMMARY:Night is not nothing
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nPeriods of obscurity can dismantle inadequ
 ate forms of knowing without yet supplying replacements.\n\nDetachment mat
 ters because even good experiences can become substitutes for the reality 
 they once mediated.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Meister Eckhart (“God is not y
 our concept”): Both thoughts make intellectual limitation productive rat
 her than merely embarrassing. The question is whether the limit invites si
 lence\, better concepts\, or transformed desire. John of the Cross's route
  is dark night\, detachment\, and transformed desire\; Meister Eckhart's i
 s detachment\, inward poverty\, and apophatic preaching.\n\nWith Abba Mose
 s (“Strength can become gentleness”): Language reaches its limit witho
 ut becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give
  way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270223@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270417T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270417T011200
SUMMARY:Structure distributes force
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nStrength can emerge from how forces are d
 istributed rather than from simply adding more material.\n\nFuller habitua
 lly moves from object to system: what matters is often the invisible distr
 ibution of forces\, resources\, and relations.\n\nSource: tensegrity and s
 tructural work.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270224@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270418T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270418T011200
SUMMARY:Small words can turn
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA modest verbal turn near the end of a poem c
 an reorganize everything that came before it.\n\nHerbert makes ordinary ob
 jects into conceptual instruments: a pulley\, collar\, altar\, window\, or
  flower can carry an argument.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of the Cross (
 “Night is not nothing”): Language reaches its limit without becoming u
 seless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give way to unsay
 ing.\n\nSource: The Temple.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270225@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270419T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270419T011200
SUMMARY:Consolation is not the goal
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nPleasant spiritual experience can become a
 nother possession if it is confused with the reality it mediates.\n\nImage
 : The traveler keeps walking after the landmarks disappear\; the lost map 
 is not yet a new map.\n\nSource: Ascent of Mount Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270226@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270420T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270420T011200
SUMMARY:Joy can be defiant
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nJoy need not result from naïveté\; it can
  be a stance maintained in full awareness of difficulty.\n\nWonder\, for C
 hesterton\, begins when existence stops being treated as the default setti
 ng of the universe.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of the Cross (“Night is n
 ot nothing”): Desire changes when its object cannot be conceptually poss
 essed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more exact.\n\nSource: Or
 thodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270227@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270421T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270421T011200
SUMMARY:Moses never arrives
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nA spiritual journey can deepen precisely be
 cause its object is inexhaustible rather than finally possessed.\n\nGregor
 y's distinctive move is to make fulfillment compatible with further desire
 . Infinity changes what 'arrival' can mean.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith G. K. Ch
 esterton (“Joy can be defiant”): Desire changes when its object cannot
  be conceptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more
  exact.\n\nWith John of the Cross (“Night is not nothing”): Gregory's 
 darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equ
 ation of obscurity with absence.\n\nSource: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270228@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270422T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270422T011200
SUMMARY:Names reveal and hide
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nA name can genuinely disclose while remaini
 ng radically inadequate to what it names.\n\nApophatic negation is not nih
 ilism. It is a discipline for refusing to confuse a successful concept wit
 h the reality it names.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“Failure can
  perform”): Kaufman's refusal to clarify the performance frame has an od
 d kinship with apophatic refusal: certainty is withheld\, though for radic
 ally different ends.\n\nSource: Divine Names.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270301@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270423T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270423T011200
SUMMARY:Forget to attend
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nDeliberate forgetting can sometimes clear t
 he field for a different mode of attention.\n\nA short word or simple desi
 re can concentrate attention more effectively than an elaborate spiritual 
 explanation.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of the Cross (“Night is not noth
 ing”): One asks what enters awareness\; the other asks which environment
  trains that awareness. The connection is ecological: perception has infra
 structure.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270302@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270424T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270424T011200
SUMMARY:Habit can divide the will
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nOne can sincerely desire change while hab
 its embody older desires strongly enough to make the will feel split.\n\nH
 is account of time begins in attention. Past and future exist for us throu
 gh present remembering and expecting.\n\nSource: Confessions\, Book VIII.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270303@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270425T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270425T011200
SUMMARY:The environment stays hidden
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nThe most powerful media effects are often d
 ifficult to notice because they become the background conditions of ordina
 ry perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Forget to attend
 ”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction 
 is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had be
 en filtering perception in the first place. Marshall McLuhan's route is me
 dia ecology and figure/ground perception\; The Cloud author's is apophatic
  contemplation and the discipline of unknowing.\n\nSource: The Medium Is t
 he Massage.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270304@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270426T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270426T011200
SUMMARY:Attention can be guarded
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nAttention is treated as something with thr
 esholds and gates\, not as a neutral spotlight one always controls.\n\nCON
 VERSATION\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“The environment stays hidden”): Bot
 h thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that o
 ne asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filter
 ing perception in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic ps
 ychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts\; Marshall McLuhan's is me
 dia ecology and figure/ground perception.\n\nSource: ascetical tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270305@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270427T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270427T011200
SUMMARY:Chance can compose
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nChance procedures can remove habits of preference 
 and allow structures the maker would not deliberately choose.\n\nCONVERSAT
 ION\nWith The Cloud author (“Forget to attend”): Cage's silence and th
 e Cloud author's unknowing both change attention by removing expected cont
 ent. One opens the ear\; the other interrupts conceptual possession.\n\nWi
 th Gregory of Nyssa (“Moses never arrives”): Both thoughts make intell
 ectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The question
  is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed d
 esire. John Cage's route is composition and disciplined listening\; Gregor
 y of Nyssa's is mystical theology and endless progress.\n\nSource: chance 
 operations and Music of Changes.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270306@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270428T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270428T011200
SUMMARY:The whole changes parts
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nIn a system\, the behavior of a part depe
 nds on relationships that disappear when the part is considered alone.\n\n
 Fuller's favorite kind of elegance is functional: more capability from les
 s matter\, less friction\, and better organization.\n\nSource: synergetics
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270307@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270429T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270429T011200
SUMMARY:Hierarchy can transmit
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nA hierarchy\, in Dionysius's ideal sense\, 
 is a pattern of receiving and transmitting illumination rather than merely
  ranking status.\n\nApophatic negation is not nihilism. It is a discipline
  for refusing to confuse a successful concept with the reality it names.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Forget to attend”): The Cloud 
 author makes a practice of the unknowing Dionysius describes: conceptual s
 ubtraction becomes something enacted by attention and desire.\n\nSource: C
 elestial Hierarchy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270308@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270430T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270430T011200
SUMMARY:Ecstasy needs fruit
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nExtraordinary experience matters less than 
 the durable transformation it produces in conduct and relation.\n\nCONVERS
 ATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Attention can be guarded”): Teresa's r
 ooms and Evagrius's thought-patterns offer two different maps of interior 
 attention: one architectural and relational\, one diagnostic and ascetic.\
 n\nSource: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270309@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270501T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270501T011200
SUMMARY:Discern the right burden
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nNot every hardship should be escape
 d\, and not every hardship should be embraced. Discernment asks what a dif
 ficulty is doing.\n\nSyncletica's asceticism is more discriminating than m
 aximalist. The question is not 'how hard can I be on myself?' but 'what is
  this practice actually producing?'\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Augustine of Hip
 po (“Habit can divide the will”): Practice reveals whether freedom is 
 merely desired or has become embodied enough to survive repetition.\n\nSou
 rce: Sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270310@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270502T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270502T011200
SUMMARY:Repetition is not return
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nTrue repetition is not simple recurrence.
  What returns can become new because the person returning has changed.\n\n
 Paradox: Repetition can be the mechanism of novelty when the returning per
 son is not the same person who first encountered the event.\n\nSource: Rep
 etition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270311@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270503T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270503T011200
SUMMARY:Attention is generosity
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nTo attend without immediately appropriating\, ca
 tegorizing\, or fixing another can itself be a form of generosity.\n\nDecr
 eation is Weil's refusal of the fantasy that the self should occupy the ce
 nter of reality.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Attention can
  be guarded”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The usefu
 l friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks 
 what had been filtering perception in the first place. Simone Weil's route
  is attention\, affliction\, force\, and decreation\; Evagrius Ponticus's 
 is ascetic psychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts.\n\nWith John
  Cage (“Chance can compose”): Weil's attention and Cage's listening me
 et in a disciplined refusal to decide too quickly what matters. Weil makes
  it ethical\; Cage makes it acoustic.\n\nQUOTATION\n“Attention is the ra
 rest and purest form of generosity.” — Simone Weil\n\nSource: essays o
 n attention.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270312@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270504T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270504T011200
SUMMARY:Use is not enjoyment
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nConfusing instrumental use with final enj
 oyment distorts both objects and relationships.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Sør
 en Kierkegaard (“Repetition is not return”): Augustine's divided will 
 and Kierkegaard's analysis of despair both make the self a task rather tha
 n a transparent possession.\n\nSource: On Christian Doctrine.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270313@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270505T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270505T011200
SUMMARY:Oppression enters vision
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nSocial domination changes imagination itself\,
  teaching people to perceive constraint as natural.\n\nSource: prophetic w
 ritings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270314@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270506T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270506T011200
SUMMARY:Every medium retrieves
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nNew media often revive something older that
  a previous environment had suppressed or made obsolete.\n\nHis basic prov
 ocation is ecological: a medium changes ratios among senses\, habits\, ins
 titutions\, and speeds before anyone debates its content.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith Simone Weil (“Attention is generosity”): One asks what enters aw
 areness\; the other asks which environment trains that awareness. The conn
 ection is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSource: Laws of Me
 dia.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270315@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270507T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270507T011200
SUMMARY:The audience is material
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nReaction is not merely a response to the work\;
  it can be one of the materials from which the work is made.\n\nCONVERSATI
 ON\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“Every medium retrieves”): McLuhan's figure
 /ground distinction becomes theatrical in Kaufman\, whose performances mak
 e the audience discover that its own expectations were part of the medium.
 \n\nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Repetition is not return”): Kaufman dest
 abilizes certainty about the frame\; Kierkegaard destabilizes certainty ab
 out borrowed conviction. Both make the observer responsible for deciding w
 here to stand.\n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270316@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270508T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270508T011200
SUMMARY:Extensions become amputations
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nAn extension of one capacity can numb or di
 minish another\; augmentation always has a sensory price.\n\nSource: Under
 standing Media.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270317@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270509T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270509T011200
SUMMARY:Inner poverty can speak
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nNeed can become perceptible only when the 
 self stops covering it with competence and self-explanation.\n\nHis langua
 ge is tactile and experiential: fire\, sweetness\, weight\, battle\, and m
 ovement give inner transformation bodily texture.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Si
 mone Weil (“Attention is generosity”): Read today's thought backward t
 hrough Simone Weil: “Attention is generosity.” Both refuse the transpa
 rent self\; identity contains rooms\, masks\, memories\, habits\, or force
 s the narrator does not fully command.\n\nSource: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270318@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270510T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270510T011200
SUMMARY:Contradiction can birth
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nSome forms of generativity arise not after cont
 radiction disappears but through the structured tension it creates.\n\nHis
  contraries are dangerous and productive at once. Contraction makes form p
 ossible\, but can harden into enclosure.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William Bla
 ke (“Oppression enters vision”): Blake read Böhme\, and the affinity 
 shows: both treat contraries and energetic tension as generative rather th
 an defects to be erased.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270319@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270511T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270511T011200
SUMMARY:Surprise keeps play alive
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nA living game must leave room for outcomes it
 s players could not fully script in advance.\n\nCarse is not contrasting s
 eriousness with frivolity. His real axis is closure versus continuation: d
 oes the activity aim to end the field\, or keep genuine possibility alive?
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“The audience is material”): Cars
 e's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strongest performance of
 ten leaves the audience without a final verdict about what game was being 
 played.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270320@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270512T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270512T011200
SUMMARY:The desert magnifies
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nReduction of external stimulation can make
  small interior movements feel larger and therefore easier to see.\n\nAnth
 ony's desert does not remove struggle\; it magnifies patterns that ordinar
 y distraction can keep blurry.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Marshall McLuhan (“
 Every medium retrieves”): ” The self is not defined in isolation. Iden
 tity emerges through relation\, projection\, communion\, dependence\, or r
 ecognition.\n\nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270321@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270513T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270513T011200
SUMMARY:Strength invites response
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nStrength differs from power when it enlarges 
 the capacity of others to answer rather than reducing them to compliance.\
 n\nCarse's language sounds light\, but it is severe about identity: titles
 \, victories\, and roles become dangerous when yesterday's win decides wha
 t tomorrow is allowed to be.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“The au
 dience is material”): Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: 
 the strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final verdic
 t about what game was being played.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270322@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270514T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270514T011200
SUMMARY:Discern before fleeing
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nThe urge to escape can itself become mater
 ial for discernment rather than an automatic instruction.\n\nSource: deser
 t tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270323@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270515T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270515T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary can answer
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nDomestic and commonplace things can become in
 tellectually and spiritually articulate under sustained attention.\n\nHerb
 ert makes ordinary objects into conceptual instruments: a pulley\, collar\
 , altar\, window\, or flower can carry an argument.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith 
 James P. Carse (“Surprise keeps play alive”): Read today's thought bac
 kward through James P. Carse: “Strength invites response.” Attention b
 ecomes relational here: what one notices depends partly on whether the oth
 er is encountered as object\, partner\, or presence.\n\nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270324@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270516T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270516T011200
SUMMARY:The child is not simpler
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nWriting for children need not flatten compl
 exity\; children can encounter mystery before they possess adult explanato
 ry language.\n\nMacDonald does not treat imagination as decorative unreali
 ty. It is a faculty by which perception can be enlarged before argument ca
 tches up.\n\nSource: essays and fiction.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270325@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270517T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270517T011200
SUMMARY:Repetition changes an image
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nReturning to the same face\, motif\, 
 or poem can make repetition cumulative rather than redundant.\n\nRossetti'
 s repeated motifs and revisions make recurrence cumulative: the returned i
 mage becomes another attempt at the same unresolved imaginative problem.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith George MacDonald (“The child is not simpler”): Ma
 cDonald's stories and Rossetti's double works refuse to resolve every imag
 e into a detachable lesson. Meaning is allowed to ripen through return.\n\
 nSource: artistic practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270326@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270518T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270518T011200
SUMMARY:Violence can disclose
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nIn O'Connor's fiction\, violence often s
 trips away a character's preferred account of reality rather than merely s
 upplying plot excitement.\n\nO'Connor's grotesque is diagnostic. Distortio
 n makes visible the spiritual disproportions that respectable realism can 
 politely conceal.\n\nSource: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270327@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270519T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270519T011200
SUMMARY:Fairy tales keep doors
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA fantastic story can preserve openings tha
 t explanation would prematurely close.\n\nMacDonald does not treat imagina
 tion as decorative unreality. It is a faculty by which perception can be e
 nlarged before argument catches up.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Dante Gabriel Ro
 ssetti (“Repetition changes an image”): MacDonald's stories and Rosset
 ti's double works refuse to resolve every image into a detachable lesson. 
 Meaning is allowed to ripen through return.\n\nSource: The Fantastic Imagi
 nation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270328@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270520T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270520T011200
SUMMARY:Taste can be a cage
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nPreference helps us navigate\, but Cage repeatedly
  asks what becomes audible when taste stops policing experience.\n\nCage's
  move is compositional rather than merely therapeutic: he changes the cond
 itions under which preference gets to decide what counts.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith George Herbert (“The ordinary can answer”): Cage and Herbert cou
 ld hardly sound less alike\, yet both make form itself carry thought: Cage
  through procedural listening\, Herbert through shaped language.\n\nSource
 : Silence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270329@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270521T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270521T011200
SUMMARY:Hope is socially dangerous
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nHope becomes political whenever it refuses 
 to let an existing order identify itself with the limits of the possible.\
 n\nMoltmann's hope is not positive thinking. It is a protest against allow
 ing the present to define the limits of the possible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWit
 h Anthony the Great (“Discern before fleeing”): Read today's thought b
 ackward through Anthony the Great: “Discern before fleeing.” Both refu
 se the transparent self\; identity contains rooms\, masks\, memories\, hab
 its\, or forces the narrator does not fully command.\n\nSource: Theology o
 f Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270330@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270522T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270522T011200
SUMMARY:A center can be nowhere
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThinking about infinity can unsettle ordina
 ry spatial intuitions about center\, boundary\, and location.\n\nCusa uses
  precision to discover the limits of precision. Better measurement can inc
 rease knowledge and clarify what measurement cannot reach.\n\nSource: cosm
 ological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270331@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270523T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270523T011200
SUMMARY:Complaint can become prayer
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nA poem can begin in resistance\, irritation\,
  or argument and let that movement itself become a form of address.\n\nCON
 VERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Taste can be a cage”): Cage and Herbert co
 uld hardly sound less alike\, yet both make form itself carry thought: Cag
 e through procedural listening\, Herbert through shaped language.\n\nSourc
 e: The Temple.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270401@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270524T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270524T011200
SUMMARY:A violent past can change
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA person's history need not be denied in order fo
 r transformation to be real\; the past can remain part of the story withou
 t dictating its end.\n\nMoses's own story gives these sayings weight: tran
 sformation does not require pretending the earlier self never existed.\n\n
 CONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“Violence can disclose”): O'Co
 nnor and Abba Moses distrust moral superiority. One uses grotesque fiction
 \, the other comic desert gestures\, to expose the person who can see ever
 yone else's fault.\n\nSource: Moses's desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270402@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270525T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270525T011200
SUMMARY:Listening has no center
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nA field of sound need not have a privileged focal 
 point. Attention can move without requiring hierarchy.\n\nFor Cage\, relin
 quishing control is itself a designed procedure. Chance and silence are no
 t vagueness\; they are ways of admitting events the composer would otherwi
 se exclude.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith George Herbert (“Complaint can become 
 prayer”): Cage and Herbert could hardly sound less alike\, yet both make
  form itself carry thought: Cage through procedural listening\, Herbert th
 rough shaped language.\n\nSource: experimental music practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270403@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270526T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270526T011200
SUMMARY:Judgment can soften
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nThe desire to judge another can be displace
 d by awareness of shared fragility.\n\nIsaac's tenderness is not softness 
 of judgment so much as a refusal to reduce a creature to its offense.\n\nC
 ONVERSATION\nWith George MacDonald (“Fairy tales keep doors”): Desire 
 colors the image\, while the image educates desire. Each can quietly teach
  the other what to want.\n\nWith Abba Moses (“A violent past can change
 ”): Moses makes judgment ridiculous by showing the judge's own failures 
 leaking behind him. Isaac goes further by replacing accounting with expans
 ive mercy.\n\nSource: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270404@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270527T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270527T011200
SUMMARY:Tradition gives the dead votes
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nTradition can be understood as extending pa
 rticipation across generations rather than letting the present monopolize 
 authority.\n\nChesterton uses paradox to make the overfamiliar strange eno
 ugh to see again.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“Judgment can 
 soften”): Desire colors the image\, while the image educates desire. Eac
 h can quietly teach the other what to want.\n\nSource: Orthodoxy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270405@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270528T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270528T011200
SUMMARY:The line can be prophetic
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nGraphic form\, text\, and image can participat
 e in one visionary act rather than serving separate illustrative functions
 .\n\nSource: illuminated books.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270406@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270529T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270529T011200
SUMMARY:Despair can look normal
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA person can function successfully while 
 remaining estranged from the self they are becoming.\n\nParadox: The self 
 becomes itself not by eliminating dependence\, but by relating rightly to 
 conditions it did not choose.\n\nSource: The Sickness Unto Death.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270407@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270530T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270530T011200
SUMMARY:A polygon seeks the circle
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThe image of a many-sided polygon approachi
 ng a circle shows how approximation can increase without becoming identity
 .\n\nDistinction: Approximation can become indefinitely better without bec
 oming identity.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Listening has no cente
 r”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction
  is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had b
 een filtering perception in the first place. Nicholas of Cusa's route is l
 earned ignorance\, infinity\, and mathematical metaphor\; John Cage's is c
 omposition and disciplined listening.\n\nSource: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270408@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270531T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270531T011200
SUMMARY:Practice alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nAscetic practice changes what appears 
 desirable\, possible\, and real\, not merely what behavior is permitted.\n
 \nFor Maximus\, love is epistemic as well as moral: distorted relation can
  become distorted perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“J
 udgment can soften”): Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic commun
 ion meet in a refusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the pri
 vate self.\n\nSource: Four Hundred Texts on Love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270409@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270601T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270601T011200
SUMMARY:Reading can transform
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nInterpretation is not only extraction of information.
  The reader can be changed by the act of reading.\n\nProvocation: A readin
 g that leaves the reader entirely unchanged may have extracted information
  while missing the kind of truth Origen seeks.\n\nSource: biblical exegesi
 s.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270410@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270602T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270602T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\n\nA crowd can lend borrowed conviction. Bec
 oming a self requires personally inhabiting what one takes to be true.\n\n
 CONVERSATION\nWith William Blake (“The line can be prophetic”): Read t
 oday's thought backward through William Blake: “The line can be propheti
 c.” Both refuse the transparent self\; identity contains rooms\, masks\,
  memories\, habits\, or forces the narrator does not fully command.\n\nSou
 rce: writings on the single individual.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270411@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270603T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270603T011200
SUMMARY:A cell mirrors the mind
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nConfinement can make inner agitatio
 n more visible because fewer external changes are available to disguise it
 .\n\nImage: A new cell can still contain the old weather if the storm was 
 carried inside.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Søren Kierkegaard (“Despair can l
 ook normal”): Practice turns identity from a description into an experim
 ent. Repetition reveals what the self can actually sustain.\n\nSource: des
 ert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270412@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270604T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270604T011200
SUMMARY:Power ends play
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nPower can force an outcome\, but forcing an o
 utcome is different from sustaining a field in which genuine play remains 
 possible.\n\nCarse treats rules as tools of a game rather than sacred furn
 iture. In infinite play\, even the rules remain answerable to the continua
 tion of play.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270413@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270605T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270605T011200
SUMMARY:Noise can become identity
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nConstant stimulation can become a defense agai
 nst encountering the self that appears when performance stops.\n\nHis soli
 tude is relational rather than escapist: withdrawal matters insofar as it 
 clears away the performance that blocks genuine encounter.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Syncletica of Alexandria (“A cell mirrors the mind”): Practice 
 turns identity from a description into an experiment. Repetition reveals w
 hat the self can actually sustain.\n\nSource: contemplative writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270414@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270606T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270606T011200
SUMMARY:Patience can be active
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nPatience need not mean passivity. I
 t can be the disciplined refusal to let discomfort dictate the next move.\
 n\nSyncletica's asceticism is more discriminating than maximalist. The que
 stion is not 'how hard can I be on myself?' but 'what is this practice act
 ually producing?'\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (“Noise can become
  identity”): Freedom is not external to identity\; repeated choices part
 icipate in building the self that will make the next choice.\n\nSource: sa
 yings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270415@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270607T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270607T011200
SUMMARY:Work narrows the horizon
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nA life understood only through work and utility c
 an conceal dimensions of existence disclosed by play\, love\, death\, and 
 festival.\n\nHis 'world' is not the biggest object. It is the horizon in w
 hich objects\, tasks\, and persons can appear at all.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWit
 h James P. Carse (“Power ends play”): Carse and Fink take play serious
 ly enough to make it philosophically revealing. Carse emphasizes continuat
 ion\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making character of play itself.\n
 \nSource: existential phenomenology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270416@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270608T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270608T011200
SUMMARY:Punishment need not define
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nDivine judgment can be interpreted within a
  larger horizon of healing rather than as an ultimate delight in retributi
 on.\n\nDistinction: Mercy is not the denial that harm occurred\; it is the
  refusal to let the harm exhaust the identity of the person who caused it.
 \n\nSource: Isaac's eschatological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270417@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270609T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270609T011200
SUMMARY:Edges can gain thickness
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nA living boundary often becomes a zone
  with its own structure rather than a thin line separating two domains.\n\
 nAlexander's language of 'life' is not just aesthetic mood. He is making a
  structural claim about how centers reinforce one another and how local ch
 anges affect a larger field.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Eugen Fink (“Work nar
 rows the horizon”): Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of center
 s resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order le
 ts a thing be what it is.\n\nSource: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270418@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270610T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270610T011200
SUMMARY:Joy can anticipate truth
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nCelebration can be anticipatory rather than
  escapist: a bodily rehearsal of a world not yet fully present.\n\nMoltman
 n refuses consolation that closes history too soon. Suffering stays real\,
  but it is denied the right to become the final metaphysical fact.\n\nCONV
 ERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“Power ends play”): Moltmann's future-o
 riented hope and Carse's infinite play both resist closure. One is theolog
 ical and eschatological\; the other ludic\, but both ask whether the futur
 e remains genuinely open.\n\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“Punishment need not
  define”): Both refuse to let a present failure become an exhaustive ide
 ntity. Mercy does it interpersonally\; hope does it temporally.\n\nSource:
  Theology of Play and related essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270419@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270611T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270611T011200
SUMMARY:Interior space is large
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nThe inner life can be approached as a compl
 ex architecture rather than a single stream of private feeling.\n\nTeresa 
 treats prayer more as relation than technique. Friendship changes the logi
 c from performance to presence.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Syncletica of Alexan
 dria (“Patience can be active”): Freedom is not external to identity\;
  repeated choices participate in building the self that will make the next
  choice.\n\nSource: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270420@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270612T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270612T011200
SUMMARY:Systems hide in objects
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nAn object often makes sense only as part 
 of a larger system of flows\, constraints\, and relations.\n\nFuller's fav
 orite kind of elegance is functional: more capability from less matter\, l
 ess friction\, and better organization.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christopher 
 Alexander (“Edges can gain thickness”): Alexander and Fuller both thin
 k relationally\, but their temperaments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward s
 ystem and efficiency\; Alexander keeps asking whether the resulting form a
 ctually feels more alive.\n\nSource: synergetics and design science.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270421@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270613T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270613T011200
SUMMARY:Watch before arguing
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nA thought can be observed as it forms\, be
 fore one accepts its framing and begins debating inside it.\n\nDistinction
 : A thought and the truth of what the thought says are different questions
 . Evagrius often begins with the first.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster 
 Fuller (“Systems hide in objects”): One asks what enters awareness\; t
 he other asks which environment trains that awareness. The connection is e
 cological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nWith Teresa of Ávila (“Int
 erior space is large”): Teresa's rooms and Evagrius's thought-patterns o
 ffer two different maps of interior attention: one architectural and relat
 ional\, one diagnostic and ascetic.\n\nSource: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270422@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270614T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270614T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can become clutter
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nMore concepts are not always more contact\;
  intellectual accumulation can become a defense against encounter.\n\nImag
 e: A single short word rises through a cloud of concepts like a hand reach
 ing where the eye cannot see.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270423@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270615T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270615T011200
SUMMARY:Do not judge too quickly
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nAwareness of one's own disorder can interrupt the
  pleasure of judging another person's visible failure.\n\nImage: Moses wal
 ks into judgment carrying a leaking bag of sand: his own faults trail behi
 nd him while he is asked to inspect someone else's.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith 
 The Cloud author (“Knowing can become clutter”): Language reaches its 
 limit without becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying trul
 y must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270424@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270616T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270616T011200
SUMMARY:Love joins without blur
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nUnion need not abolish particularity. 
 Relation can intensify difference while overcoming estrangement.\n\nCONVER
 SATION\nWith Christopher Alexander (“Edges can gain thickness”): Alexa
 nder's wholeness and Maximus's communion share a structural intuition: uni
 ty is strongest when differences are related without being flattened.\n\nS
 ource: Ambigua.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270425@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270617T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270617T011200
SUMMARY:Waiting changes desire
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nDelay can transform not only whether a de
 sire is fulfilled but what the desiring person becomes.\n\nWaiting in Ross
 etti is transformative. Delay does not merely postpone fulfillment\; it ch
 anges the desiring person.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Know
 ing can become clutter”): Desire changes when its object cannot be conce
 ptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or make it more exact.\n
 \nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270426@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270618T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270618T011200
SUMMARY:Tears enlarge perception
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompunction is not merely private sadness\;
  it can become a way of perceiving the vulnerability of the whole creation
 .\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Maximus the Confessor (“Love joins without blur
 ”): Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a refu
 sal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.\n\nSour
 ce: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270427@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270619T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270619T011200
SUMMARY:Restlessness can guide
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nRestlessness is not only a defect to supp
 ress\; it can reveal that a desire has mistaken a finite object for its fi
 nal home.\n\nHis account of time begins in attention. Past and future exis
 t for us through present remembering and expecting.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith 
 Christina Rossetti (“Waiting changes desire”): Both thoughts treat des
 ire as educable. Wanting is not a fixed appetite but something altered by 
 waiting\, loss\, attention\, and the object it approaches. Augustine of Hi
 ppo's route is memory\, desire\, time\, and the divided will\; Christina R
 ossetti's is devotional poetry\, desire\, restraint\, and renunciation.\n\
 nWith The Cloud author (“Knowing can become clutter”): Desire changes 
 when its object cannot be conceptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify l
 onging or make it more exact.\n\nQUOTATION\n“Our heart is restless until
  it rests in you.” — Augustine\, Confessions\n\nSource: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270428@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270620T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270620T011200
SUMMARY:Silence can carry speech
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nWhat a poem withholds can structure meani
 ng as strongly as what it states.\n\nHer restraint is formally active: wha
 t is withheld often concentrates the force of what remains.\n\nSource: poe
 try.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270429@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270621T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270621T011200
SUMMARY:Attachment can hide
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nEven attachment to good experiences can na
 rrow perception when the experience becomes an end in itself.\n\nCONVERSAT
 ION\nWith Augustine of Hippo (“Restlessness can guide”): Both thoughts
  treat desire as educable. Wanting is not a fixed appetite but something a
 ltered by waiting\, loss\, attention\, and the object it approaches. John 
 of the Cross's route is dark night\, detachment\, and transformed desire\;
  Augustine of Hippo's is memory\, desire\, time\, and the divided will.\n\
 nWith Christina Rossetti (“Waiting changes desire”): Rossetti's renunc
 iation and John's night both ask whether deprivation can purify desire rat
 her than merely frustrate it. One works poetically\; the other\, mysticall
 y.\n\nSource: Ascent of Mount Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270430@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270622T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270622T011200
SUMMARY:Festival changes time
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nFestival interrupts instrumental time. Its value 
 lies partly in suspending the demand that every moment justify itself by w
 hat it produces.\n\nFink treats play cosmologically rather than recreation
 ally: play discloses something about world-making itself.\n\nSource: writi
 ngs on play and world.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270501@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270623T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270623T011200
SUMMARY:Desire gives weight
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nWhat we love gives direction and gravity 
 to attention\, memory\, and action.\n\nQUOTATION\n“My weight is my love.
 ” — Augustine\, Confessions XIII\n\nSource: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270502@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270624T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270624T011200
SUMMARY:Compassion reaches animals
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nIsaac's famous image of the merciful heart 
 includes sorrow for humans\, animals\, and all creation.\n\nIsaac's tender
 ness is not softness of judgment so much as a refusal to reduce a creature
  to its offense.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Augustine of Hippo (“Restlessness
  can guide”): Desire colors the image\, while the image educates desire.
  Each can quietly teach the other what to want.\n\nSource: Ascetical Homil
 ies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270503@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270625T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270625T011200
SUMMARY:Growth can stay coherent
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nComplex order can emerge incrementally
  when each step preserves and strengthens what is already alive.\n\nAlexan
 der's language of 'life' is not just aesthetic mood. He is making a struct
 ural claim about how centers reinforce one another and how local changes a
 ffect a larger field.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Eugen Fink (“Festival change
 s time”): Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers resist t
 reating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing
  be what it is.\n\nSource: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270504@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270626T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270626T011200
SUMMARY:Restraint can intensify
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nFormal and emotional restraint can concen
 trate rather than diminish expressive force.\n\nWaiting in Rossetti is tra
 nsformative. Delay does not merely postpone fulfillment\; it changes the d
 esiring person.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of the Cross (“Attachment can
  hide”): Rossetti's renunciation and John's night both ask whether depri
 vation can purify desire rather than merely frustrate it. One works poetic
 ally\; the other\, mystically.\n\nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270505@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270627T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270627T011200
SUMMARY:Infinity changes desire
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nIf the good is infinite\, desire need not e
 nd in possession\; desire itself can become a form of participation.\n\nQu
 estion: What kind of good would have to be finite for possession to end de
 sire?\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John of the Cross (“Attachment can hide”):
  Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both refuse t
 he easy equation of obscurity with absence.\n\nSource: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270506@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270628T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270628T011200
SUMMARY:Affliction changes scale
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nExtreme suffering can alter the very structure t
 hrough which a person experiences time\, self\, and world.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“Compassion reaches animals”): Weil and Isaac
  stretch attention beyond the self's preferred circle. Weil emphasizes the
  reality of the afflicted other\; Isaac lets compassion widen toward all c
 reation.\n\nSource: writings on affliction.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270507@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270629T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270629T011200
SUMMARY:Make the next small step
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nLarge coherent structures can arise fr
 om many local transformations when each step repairs the whole instead of 
 imposing a complete blueprint.\n\nAlexander distrusts designs that are cor
 rect in isolation but dead in context. The unit of judgment is usually the
  relation\, not the object by itself.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Eugen Fink (
 “Festival changes time”): Fink's world-horizon and Alexander's field o
 f centers resist treating things as isolated units. Each asks what larger 
 order lets a thing be what it is.\n\nSource: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270508@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270630T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270630T011200
SUMMARY:Time lives in attention
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nPast and future are encountered through p
 resent acts of memory and expectation.\n\nDesire is gravitational in Augus
 tine: what one loves quietly organizes what becomes salient\, possible\, a
 nd worth choosing.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christina Rossetti (“Restraint 
 can intensify”): Waiting does more than postpone satisfaction\; it alter
 s the person who desires and therefore alters the meaning of fulfillment.\
 n\nSource: Confessions\, Book XI.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270509@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270701T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270701T011200
SUMMARY:Fire can purify
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nImages of divine fire can be read as transformative r
 ather than merely retaliatory.\n\nDistinction: Multiple senses do not mean
  anything goes. Origen's richer readings remain ordered toward transformat
 ion and coherence.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Simone Weil (“Affliction change
 s scale”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful fr
 iction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what
  had been filtering perception in the first place. Origen's route is a dif
 ferent intellectual tradition\; Simone Weil's is attention\, affliction\, 
 force\, and decreation.\n\nSource: homilies and theological writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270510@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270702T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270702T011200
SUMMARY:Tools can reverse ends
DESCRIPTION:Ivan Illich\n\nTools and institutions can pass a threshold wher
 e producing more of a service begins to frustrate the human good they were
  built to serve.\n\nIllich's signature question is threshold: when does a 
 tool that extends human capacity begin producing dependence instead?\n\nCO
 NVERSATION\nWith Origen (“Fire can purify”): One asks what enters awar
 eness\; the other asks which environment trains that awareness. The connec
 tion is ecological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nWith Gregory of Nyss
 a (“Infinity changes desire”): Read today's thought backward through G
 regory of Nyssa: “Infinity changes desire.” Both ask whether a structu
 re serves life or quietly reorganizes life around the needs of the structu
 re.\n\nSource: Tools for Conviviality\; Medical Nemesis.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270511@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270703T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270703T011200
SUMMARY:Imagination rehearses freedom
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nImagined worlds can loosen the sense that p
 resent arrangements exhaust the forms life might take.\n\nMacDonald does n
 ot treat imagination as decorative unreality. It is a faculty by which per
 ception can be enlarged before argument catches up.\n\nSource: fantasy wri
 tings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270512@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270704T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270704T011200
SUMMARY:The heart can widen
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInterior transformation can enlarge the ca
 pacity to hold others rather than merely perfect a private self.\n\nHis la
 nguage is tactile and experiential: fire\, sweetness\, weight\, battle\, a
 nd movement give inner transformation bodily texture.\n\nSource: Macarian 
 Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270513@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270705T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270705T011200
SUMMARY:A tale should breathe
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nA story can be damaged by over-explanation 
 when commentary closes the imaginative space the tale created.\n\nMacDonal
 d trusts stories to awaken capacities in the reader rather than merely dep
 osit conclusions.\n\nSource: The Fantastic Imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270514@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270706T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270706T011200
SUMMARY:Stay long enough to see
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nRemaining with a place\, practice\, or dif
 ficulty can reveal patterns that novelty continually resets before they be
 come legible.\n\nStaying put becomes diagnostic when novelty has been func
 tioning as a reset button for the same unresolved pattern.\n\nSource: sayi
 ngs tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270515@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270707T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270707T011200
SUMMARY:Reality can be rehearsed
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nRepeated performance can make an invented situa
 tion socially real enough that its consequences no longer depend on the or
 iginal fiction.\n\nKaufman's ambiguity is not simply trickery. By refusing
  to stabilize the frame\, he turns interpretation into part of the event.\
 n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270516@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270708T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270708T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness gives light contour
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nLight becomes manifest against differentiation 
 and resistance\; pure undifferentiated light would disclose no form.\n\nLi
 ght\, for Böhme\, needs distinction in order to appear as anything at all
 .\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270517@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270709T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270709T011200
SUMMARY:Mortality sharpens beauty
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nBeauty can intensify under awareness that
  it cannot be possessed indefinitely.\n\nWaiting in Rossetti is transforma
 tive. Delay does not merely postpone fulfillment\; it changes the desiring
  person.\n\nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270518@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270710T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270710T011200
SUMMARY:A boundary can move
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nFinite play protects boundaries\; infinite pl
 ay can treat boundaries as provisional features of an ongoing field.\n\nCa
 rse is not contrasting seriousness with frivolity. His real axis is closur
 e versus continuation: does the activity aim to end the field\, or keep ge
 nuine possibility alive?\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Andy Kaufman (“Reality ca
 n be rehearsed”): Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the 
 strongest performance often leaves the audience without a final verdict ab
 out what game was being played.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270519@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270711T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270711T011200
SUMMARY:The map may need burning
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nA guide useful for one stage can become an
  obstacle when mistaken for the terrain itself.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Geor
 ge MacDonald (“A tale should breathe”): Desire colors the image\, whil
 e the image educates desire. Each can quietly teach the other what to want
 .\n\nWith Christina Rossetti (“Mortality sharpens beauty”): Rossetti's
  renunciation and John's detachment ask whether desire can be purified by 
 relinquishment\, though their poetic and mystical registers remain distinc
 t.\n\nSource: Ascent of Mount Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270520@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270712T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270712T011200
SUMMARY:Mercy can interrupt law
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nRules matter\, but the desert tradition repeatedl
 y asks whether applying a rule in this moment serves restoration or self-r
 ighteousness.\n\nSource: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270521@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270713T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270713T011200
SUMMARY:Revision becomes a second work
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nA heavily revised work may cease to b
 e merely corrected and become a different solution to the same imaginative
  problem.\n\nRossetti often lets image and poem answer one another rather 
 than assigning one to illustrate the other.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christin
 a Rossetti (“Mortality sharpens beauty”): The Rossettis share intense 
 symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire saturate the im
 age while Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, waiting\, 
 and renunciation.\n\nSource: poetic practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270522@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270714T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270714T011200
SUMMARY:The future can reorder love
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nA hoped-for future can alter what present
  desire is willing to surrender.\n\nChristina Rossetti gives refusal a pos
 itive form. Renunciation can reorganize desire rather than merely deprive 
 it.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christina Rossetti (“Mortality sharpens beauty
 ”): The Rossettis share intense symbolic imagination\, but Dante Gabriel
  often lets desire saturate the image while Christina repeatedly tests des
 ire through restraint\, waiting\, and renunciation.\n\nWith John of the Cr
 oss (“The map may need burning”): Rossetti's renunciation and John's n
 ight both ask whether deprivation can purify desire rather than merely fru
 strate it. One works poetically\; the other\, mystically.\n\nSource: devot
 ional poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270523@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270715T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270715T011200
SUMMARY:Let intention loosen
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nReducing control does not mean abandoning craft. I
 t can mean designing procedures that admit events the ego would have exclu
 ded.\n\nCage keeps shifting attention from expression to occurrence. The i
 nteresting question is often not 'what does this mean?' but 'what is actua
 lly happening when I stop filtering it?'\n\nSource: chance procedures.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270524@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270716T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270716T011200
SUMMARY:Freedom risks selfhood
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nSelfhood requires a kind of contraction and dif
 ferentiation\, but that same movement can harden into isolation.\n\nSource
 : theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270525@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270717T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270717T011200
SUMMARY:Mercy exceeds measure
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompassion can refuse the arithmetic by whi
 ch worthiness is calculated.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“A bo
 undary can move”): Carse's “A boundary can move.” Attention becomes 
 relational here: what one notices depends partly on whether the other is e
 ncountered as object\, partner\, or presence.\n\nWith Abba Moses (“Mercy
  can interrupt law”): Moses makes judgment ridiculous by showing the jud
 ge's own failures leaking behind him. Isaac goes further by replacing acco
 unting with expansive mercy.\n\nSource: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270526@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270718T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270718T011200
SUMMARY:Text can frame vision
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nA sonnet attached to an image can red
 irect the viewer's attention without exhausting the image's ambiguity.\n\n
 Rossetti's repeated motifs and revisions make recurrence cumulative: the r
 eturned image becomes another attempt at the same unresolved imaginative p
 roblem.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christina Rossetti (“The future can reorde
 r love”): Dante Gabriel often lets desire accumulate around an image\; C
 hristina repeatedly asks what happens when desire waits or refuses possess
 ion. The siblings give desire two different temporalities.\n\nSource: The 
 House of Life and related works.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270527@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270719T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270719T011200
SUMMARY:Prototype the future
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nA working artifact can sometimes test a p
 ossibility more honestly than argument alone.\n\nFuller's favorite kind of
  elegance is functional: more capability from less matter\, less friction\
 , and better organization.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Dante Gabriel Rossetti (
 “Revision becomes a second work”): Waiting does more than postpone sat
 isfaction\; it alters the person who desires and therefore alters the mean
 ing of fulfillment.\n\nSource: design practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270528@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270720T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270720T011200
SUMMARY:Sin is not the last fact
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nJulian refuses to deny sin while also refu
 sing to treat it as the ultimate interpretive key to reality.\n\nProvocati
 on: Notice how quickly judgment makes the judge feel metaphysically separa
 te from the judged.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“Mercy excee
 ds measure”): Julian and Isaac share a startling confidence in mercy wit
 hout making suffering imaginary. Both press compassion beyond ordinary pun
 itive accounting.\n\nSource: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270529@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270721T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270721T011200
SUMMARY:A face can become emblem
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nPortraiture can move from likeness to
 ward symbolic concentration\, making a face carry more than biography.\n\n
 His beauty is rarely innocent of fixation. Desire saturates the scene and 
 changes what the scene can mean.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christina Rossetti 
 (“The future can reorder love”): The Rossettis share intense symbolic 
 imagination\, but Dante Gabriel often lets desire saturate the image while
  Christina repeatedly tests desire through restraint\, waiting\, and renun
 ciation.\n\nSource: painting practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270530@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270722T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270722T011200
SUMMARY:Meaning can ripen later
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nAn image encountered before it is understoo
 d may keep working until later experience makes it newly intelligible.\n\n
 MacDonald does not treat imagination as decorative unreality. It is a facu
 lty by which perception can be enlarged before argument catches up.\n\nSou
 rce: essays on imagination.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270531@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270723T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270723T011200
SUMMARY:Souls are educable
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nSpiritual life can be imagined pedagogically: error\,
  correction\, purification\, and growth rather than static classification.
 \n\nImage: The literal text is a doorway\, not a disposable shell: one ent
 ers through it\, but need not remain standing in the threshold.\n\nSource:
  homilies and speculative theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270601@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270724T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270724T011200
SUMMARY:Why matters less than how
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nAn action done from freedom can differ radic
 ally from the same external action done from possessiveness or self-displa
 y.\n\nHis detachment is not numbness. It is freedom from the demand that r
 eality arrive in the form the ego has already approved.\n\nSource: sermons
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270602@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270725T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270725T011200
SUMMARY:Space can be positive
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nEmpty space becomes powerful when it h
 as definite shape and presence rather than remaining leftover area between
  objects.\n\nAlexander distrusts designs that are correct in isolation but
  dead in context. The unit of judgment is usually the relation\, not the o
 bject by itself.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster Fuller (“Prototype th
 e future”): Alexander and Fuller both think relationally\, but their tem
 peraments diverge. Fuller abstracts toward system and efficiency\; Alexand
 er keeps asking whether the resulting form actually feels more alive.\n\nS
 ource: A Pattern Language\; The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270603@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270726T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270726T011200
SUMMARY:Grace is not niceness
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nGrace does not necessarily preserve dign
 ity\, comfort\, or self-image. Its arrival may feel like catastrophe to th
 e person being changed.\n\nO'Connor keeps the comic and the terrible close
  together. Laughter can expose self-deception more efficiently than solemn
  moral instruction.\n\nSource: fiction.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270604@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270727T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270727T011200
SUMMARY:Comparison distorts effort
DESCRIPTION:Syncletica of Alexandria\n\nAnother person's discipline can bec
 ome a distraction when copied without regard to one's own capacities and t
 emptations.\n\nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270605@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270728T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270728T011200
SUMMARY:Vision needs distance
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nA writer may need enough distance from f
 amiliar surroundings to perceive their peculiar structure rather than repr
 oduce local clichés.\n\nSource: Mystery and Manners.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270606@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270729T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270729T011200
SUMMARY:The past can be invented
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nHistorical imagery can be less a reco
 nstruction of the past than a newly made symbolic environment.\n\nHis beau
 ty is rarely innocent of fixation. Desire saturates the scene and changes 
 what the scene can mean.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Flannery O’Connor (“Gra
 ce is not niceness”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. Th
 e useful friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the othe
 r asks what had been filtering perception in the first place. Dante Gabrie
 l Rossetti's route is Pre-Raphaelite image\, poetry\, desire\, and revisio
 n\; Flannery O’Connor's is grotesque fiction and a Catholic theology of 
 grace.\n\nSource: Pre-Raphaelite practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270607@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270730T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270730T011200
SUMMARY:Love needs no audience
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nAn action performed for recognition differs in
 wardly from one whose value does not depend on being witnessed.\n\nMerton'
 s false self is not merely dishonesty. It is the identity assembled from s
 tatus\, comparison\, performance\, and reflected approval.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Meister Eckhart (“Why matters less than how”): Merton's false s
 elf and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspicion of the identity that mu
 st constantly possess\, perform\, and defend itself.\n\nSource: New Seeds 
 of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270608@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270731T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270731T011200
SUMMARY:Titles freeze the past
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nA title names a completed victory. When ident
 ity rests on titles\, the past begins governing what one is allowed to bec
 ome.\n\nCarse treats rules as tools of a game rather than sacred furniture
 . In infinite play\, even the rules remain answerable to the continuation 
 of play.\n\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270609@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270801T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270801T011200
SUMMARY:Clarity follows simplification
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nReducing compulsive mental traffic can mak
 e perception more exact rather than merely more peaceful.\n\nStillness for
  Evagrius is trained clarity. The goal is not blankness\, but freedom from
  being involuntarily recruited by every mental event.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWit
 h Syncletica of Alexandria (“Comparison distorts effort”): Read today'
 s thought backward through Syncletica of Alexandria: “Comparison distort
 s effort.” Attention becomes relational here: what one notices depends p
 artly on whether the other is encountered as object\, partner\, or presenc
 e.\n\nWith Dante Gabriel Rossetti (“The past can be invented”): Both t
 houghts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one 
 asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering
  perception in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic psych
 ology and the analysis of recurring thoughts\; Dante Gabriel Rossetti's is
  Pre-Raphaelite image\, poetry\, desire\, and revision.\n\nSource: Praktik
 os.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270610@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270802T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270802T011200
SUMMARY:Image answers poem
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n\nWhen image and poem address the same 
 subject\, neither need merely illustrate the other\; each can alter how th
 e other is read.\n\nRossetti often lets image and poem answer one another 
 rather than assigning one to illustrate the other.\n\nSource: double works
  and sonnet-painting relations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270611@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270803T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270803T011200
SUMMARY:Play makes a world
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nPlay is not merely an escape from reality. It ope
 ns a distinctive world with its own seriousness\, rules\, and horizon.\n\n
 Fink's great existential phenomena—play\, love\, death\, work\, dominati
 on—are ways the world becomes intelligible through lived structures.\n\n
 CONVERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“Titles freeze the past”): Carse an
 d Fink take play seriously enough to make it philosophically revealing. Ca
 rse emphasizes continuation\; Fink emphasizes the strange world-making cha
 racter of play itself.\n\nSource: Play as Symbol of the World.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270612@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270804T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270804T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can be simpler
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nA single undivided intention can sometimes 
 reach further than a proliferation of spiritual techniques.\n\nThe Cloud a
 uthor treats conceptual abundance as a possible obstacle. More thoughts ca
 n mean less contact.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270613@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270805T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270805T011200
SUMMARY:The self is received
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nThe deepest self is not manufactured by accumu
 lating distinctions\; it is discovered through a release of compulsive sel
 f-construction.\n\nMerton's silence can become social. It is valuable when
  it makes the world more perceptible\, not when it becomes a private spiri
 tual possession.\n\nSource: New Seeds of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270614@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270806T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270806T011200
SUMMARY:Seeing can be gentle
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nA severe reality need not require a severe
  mode of attention.\n\nJulian's confidence does not come from denying woun
 ds. Her distinctive move is to deny them the final interpretive authority.
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Clarity follows simplificatio
 n”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction
  is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had b
 een filtering perception in the first place. Julian of Norwich's route is 
 visionary theology of creation\, suffering\, and love\; Evagrius Ponticus'
 s is ascetic psychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts.\n\nSource:
  Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270615@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270807T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270807T011200
SUMMARY:Darkness can disclose
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nAt a limit of knowledge\, obscurity need no
 t mean absence. It can mark encounter with what exceeds comprehension.\n\n
 CONVERSATION\nWith Dante Gabriel Rossetti (“Image answers poem”): Read
  today's thought backward through Dante Gabriel Rossetti: “Image answers
  poem.” Both push language to its edge: words are necessary\, but their 
 usefulness includes revealing where words cease to be adequate.\n\nSource:
  Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270616@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270808T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270808T011200
SUMMARY:The senses have limits
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nA mode of knowing can be genuine and still
  reach a point beyond which a different mode is required.\n\nJohn's darkne
 ss is not a celebration of misery. It names the failure of old modes of kn
 owing during a transition they cannot themselves complete.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith The Cloud author (“Desire can be simpler”): Both thoughts make 
 intellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The qu
 estion is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transfo
 rmed desire. John of the Cross's route is dark night\, detachment\, and tr
 ansformed desire\; The Cloud author's is apophatic contemplation and the d
 iscipline of unknowing.\n\nWith Gregory of Nyssa (“Darkness can disclose
 ”): Gregory's darkness and John's night are not identical\, but both ref
 use the easy equation of obscurity with absence.\n\nSource: Ascent of Moun
 t Carmel.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270617@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270809T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270809T011200
SUMMARY:Community tests solitude
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nDesert spirituality is not purely solitary\; rela
 tions with other people expose what private discipline can hide.\n\nSource
 : Sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270618@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270810T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270810T011200
SUMMARY:Constraint can sing
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nFormal limits can generate invention rather t
 han merely suppress it.\n\nObservation: A tiny turn near the end of a Herb
 ert poem can retroactively reorganize the whole path by which the reader a
 rrived there.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Abba Moses (“Community tests solitud
 e”): Both resist the isolated unit. Form emerges from relations\, while 
 relation becomes perceptible through form.\n\nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270619@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270811T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270811T011200
SUMMARY:Division seeks communion
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nDifference need not culminate in separ
 ation\; distinctions can become sites of communion without being erased.\n
 \nFor Maximus\, love is epistemic as well as moral: distorted relation can
  become distorted perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Abba Moses (“Communi
 ty tests solitude”): Both thoughts resist unity by erasure. Relation bec
 omes stronger when difference survives contact. Maximus the Confessor's ro
 ute is cosmic relation\, difference\, and communion\; Abba Moses's is dese
 rt wisdom\, humility\, and anti-judgment.\n\nWith George Herbert (“Const
 raint can sing”): Both resist the isolated unit. Form emerges from relat
 ions\, while relation becomes perceptible through form.\n\nSource: Ambigua
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270620@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270812T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270812T011200
SUMMARY:The birth is inward
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\n\nEckhart repeatedly treats divine birth not o
 nly as a past event but as something that must occur in the ground of the 
 soul.\n\nEckhart pushes inward until the familiar narrated self is no long
 er the deepest ground of identity.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (
 “The self is received”): Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment 
 converge in suspicion of the identity that must constantly possess\, perfo
 rm\, and defend itself.\n\nSource: Christmas sermons.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270621@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270813T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270813T011200
SUMMARY:Solitude can join
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSolitude need not mean isolation\; it can expo
 se forms of relation obscured by constant social performance.\n\nDistincti
 on: Solitude is not social absence\; it can become a condition for less po
 ssessive relation.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Meister Eckhart (“The birth is 
 inward”): Merton's false self and Eckhart's detachment converge in suspi
 cion of the identity that must constantly possess\, perform\, and defend i
 tself.\n\nSource: contemplative writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270622@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270814T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270814T011200
SUMMARY:Absence refines desire
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nThe loss of familiar consolation can expos
 e whether desire seeks reality or merely a preferred experience of it.\n\n
 Question: Do you want the reality itself\, or the familiar experience by w
 hich you have learned to recognize it?\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Gregory of Ny
 ssa (“Darkness can disclose”): Gregory's darkness and John's night are
  not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity with absen
 ce.\n\nSource: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270623@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270815T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270815T011200
SUMMARY:A short word can gather
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nA very small prayer can concentrate desire 
 more effectively than an elaborate explanation.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John
  of the Cross (“The senses have limits”): Language reaches its limit w
 ithout becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must 
 give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270624@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270816T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270816T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty draws beyond itself
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nBeauty can awaken desire that refuses to te
 rminate in the beautiful object as possession.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John 
 of the Cross (“Absence refines desire”): Gregory's darkness and John's
  night are not identical\, but both refuse the easy equation of obscurity 
 with absence.\n\nWith The Cloud author (“A short word can gather”): La
 nguage reaches its limit without becoming useless. The interesting point i
 s where saying truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Homilies on the
  Song of Songs.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270625@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270817T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270817T011200
SUMMARY:Climb by unknowing
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nThe ascent of thought may culminate not in 
 a larger concept but in relinquishing conceptual possession.\n\nFor Dionys
 ius\, excess can look like darkness: the instrument fails not because noth
 ing is present\, but because what is present exceeds its capacity.\n\nCONV
 ERSATION\nWith Gregory of Nyssa (“Beauty draws beyond itself”): Both t
 houghts make intellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarra
 ssing. The question is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts
 \, or transformed desire. Pseudo-Dionysius's route is apophatic theology a
 nd symbolic language\; Gregory of Nyssa's is mystical theology and endless
  progress.\n\nWith Meister Eckhart (“The birth is inward”): Eckhart's 
 detachment and Dionysius's negation share an apophatic discipline: even a 
 true concept can become false in use when held as possession.\n\nSource: M
 ystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270626@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270818T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270818T011200
SUMMARY:Need is not the good
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\n\nNecessity governs much of the world\, but recogn
 izing necessity is not the same as worshiping it.\n\nHer thought is severe
  about necessity: one must see constraint exactly without confusing what i
 s necessary with what is good.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Maximus the Confessor
  (“Division seeks communion”): Mercy widens relation by refusing to re
 duce the other to the event that currently defines them.\n\nWith Thomas Me
 rton (“Solitude can join”): Weil's decreation and Merton's false self 
 both distrust the self that must remain at the center. Weil speaks through
  attention and affliction\; Merton through contemplation and performance.\
 n\nSource: Gravity and Grace.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270627@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270819T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270819T011200
SUMMARY:You can trim without loss
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\n\nEfficiency can mean preserving function w
 hile removing unnecessary mass\, friction\, or duplication.\n\nFuller habi
 tually moves from object to system: what matters is often the invisible di
 stribution of forces\, resources\, and relations.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jo
 hn of the Cross (“Absence refines desire”): Read today's thought backw
 ard through John of the Cross: “Absence refines desire.” Both refuse t
 o treat suffering as a detachable episode\; it reorganizes perception\, de
 sire\, or hope.\n\nSource: design science.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270628@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270820T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270820T011200
SUMMARY:Contraries share a room
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nHoliness and disorder can coexist in one p
 erson without either being imaginary.\n\nThe Macarian tradition permits co
 ntraries to coexist within one person without pretending either side is un
 real.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Pseudo-Dionysius (“Climb by unknowing”): L
 anguage reaches its limit without becoming useless. The interesting point 
 is where saying truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Macarian Homil
 ies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270629@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270821T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270821T011200
SUMMARY:Notice the ground
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\n\nWe habitually notice the figure and miss th
 e environment that makes the figure possible.\n\nImage: Look away from the
  picture and at the frame\, wall\, room\, and posture of the viewer. McLuh
 an keeps moving the question outward.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Buckminster Fu
 ller (“You can trim without loss”): One asks what enters awareness\; t
 he other asks which environment trains that awareness. The connection is e
 cological: perception has infrastructure.\n\nSource: figure/ground analysi
 s.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270630@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270822T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270822T011200
SUMMARY:Excess can look dark
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nWhat exceeds cognition can appear as darkne
 ss\, not because nothing is there but because the instrument is overwhelme
 d.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“A short word can gather”):
  ” Desire continues where comprehension fails\; not-knowing becomes a co
 ndition of relation rather than a reason to stop.\n\nWith Gregory of Nyssa
  (“Beauty draws beyond itself”): Both thoughts make intellectual limit
 ation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The question is whether 
 the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed desire. Pseud
 o-Dionysius's route is apophatic theology and symbolic language\; Gregory 
 of Nyssa's is mystical theology and endless progress.\n\nSource: Mystical 
 Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270701@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270823T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270823T011200
SUMMARY:Love is epistemic
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nHow one loves affects what one can kno
 w\; distorted relation can become distorted perception.\n\nCONVERSATION\nW
 ith Marshall McLuhan (“Notice the ground”): Both thoughts alter the co
 nditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks what becomes pe
 rceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perception in the f
 irst place. Maximus the Confessor's route is cosmic relation\, difference\
 , and communion\; Marshall McLuhan's is media ecology and figure/ground pe
 rception.\n\nSource: Four Hundred Texts on Love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270702@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270824T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270824T011200
SUMMARY:Suffering forbids closure
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nAny account of reality that makes suffering
  feel final has surrendered something essential to hope.\n\nFor Moltmann\,
  the future is theologically active in the present. Promise does not merel
 y predict\; it reorganizes what can be attempted now.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWit
 h Simone Weil (“Need is not the good”): Both refuse to let a present f
 ailure become an exhaustive identity. Mercy does it interpersonally\; hope
  does it temporally.\n\nSource: The Crucified God.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270703@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270825T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270825T011200
SUMMARY:A game exceeds players
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nOnce play begins\, the field can generate possibi
 lities no individual player fully controls.\n\nFink treats play cosmologic
 ally rather than recreationally: play discloses something about world-maki
 ng itself.\n\nSource: Play as Symbol of the World.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270704@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270826T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270826T011200
SUMMARY:Nothing need be added
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\n\nAn environment already contains events. Art can so
 metimes consist in changing the conditions of attention rather than adding
  an object.\n\nCage's move is compositional rather than merely therapeutic
 : he changes the conditions under which preference gets to decide what cou
 nts.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Maximus the Confessor (“Love is epistemic”)
 : Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is t
 hat one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been f
 iltering perception in the first place. John Cage's route is composition a
 nd disciplined listening\; Maximus the Confessor's is cosmic relation\, di
 fference\, and communion.\n\nSource: 4′33″ and later work.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270705@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270827T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270827T011200
SUMMARY:Death frames the world
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nMortality is not simply one event among others\; 
 awareness of death alters how the whole field of life appears.\n\nFink's g
 reat existential phenomena—play\, love\, death\, work\, domination—are
  ways the world becomes intelligible through lived structures.\n\nSource: 
 Existence and Coexistence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270706@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270828T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270828T011200
SUMMARY:Poetry precedes proof
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nSome insight arrives before discursive proo
 f\, through intuition embodied in image\, rhythm\, and making.\n\nImage: T
 hink of a craftsman's judgment arriving in the gesture before it arrives i
 n the sentence.\n\nSource: Creative Intuition in Art and Poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270707@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270829T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270829T011200
SUMMARY:Light needs distinction
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nManifestation requires difference: an undiffere
 ntiated brightness would reveal nothing in particular.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWi
 th Pseudo-Dionysius (“Excess can look dark”): Both thoughts make intel
 lectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The questio
 n is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transformed 
 desire. Jakob Böhme's route is theosophical cosmology\, tension\, and man
 ifestation\; Pseudo-Dionysius's is apophatic theology and symbolic languag
 e.\n\nWith Jacques Maritain (“Poetry precedes proof”): An image can ho
 ld what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of remaining pr
 ecise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270708@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270830T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270830T011200
SUMMARY:Name the thought-pattern
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nSeeing a thought as a recurring kind of ev
 ent can loosen its claim to be simply identical with reality.\n\nCONVERSAT
 ION\nWith Maximus the Confessor (“Love is epistemic”): Both thoughts a
 lter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks what
  becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering percepti
 on in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic psychology and
  the analysis of recurring thoughts\; Maximus the Confessor's is cosmic re
 lation\, difference\, and communion.\n\nSource: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270709@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270831T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270831T011200
SUMMARY:A world fits a grain
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nScale in imagination is unstable\; the minute 
 can become an aperture onto the immense.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jakob Böhm
 e (“Light needs distinction”): Blake read Böhme\, and the affinity sh
 ows: both treat contraries and energetic tension as generative rather than
  defects to be erased.\n\nQUOTATION\n“To see a World in a Grain of Sand
 ” — William Blake\n\nSource: Auguries of Innocence.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270710@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270901T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270901T011200
SUMMARY:Fantasy can tell truth
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\n\nDeparting from ordinary realism can reveal 
 structures of desire and moral imagination that literal description misses
 .\n\nMacDonald does not treat imagination as decorative unreality. It is a
  faculty by which perception can be enlarged before argument catches up.\n
 \nCONVERSATION\nWith William Blake (“A world fits a grain”): MacDonald
  and Blake refuse to treat imagination as decorative unreality. For both\,
  imaginative vision can expose possibilities ordinary habits of perception
  conceal.\n\nSource: fantasy writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270711@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270902T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270902T011200
SUMMARY:Stillness needs training
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nQuiet is not merely an absence of activity
 . It can require disciplined attention to what otherwise seizes the mind.\
 n\nCONVERSATION\nWith John Cage (“Nothing need be added”): Both though
 ts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful friction is that one asks 
 what becomes perceptible while the other asks what had been filtering perc
 eption in the first place. Evagrius Ponticus's route is ascetic psychology
  and the analysis of recurring thoughts\; John Cage's is composition and d
 isciplined listening.\n\nSource: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270712@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270903T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270903T011200
SUMMARY:The person exceeds the role
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nA person participates in communities withou
 t being reducible to function\, class\, usefulness\, or collective identit
 y.\n\nMaritain insists that artistic truth has its own mode. A poem need n
 ot become a syllogism in order to know something.\n\nSource: The Person an
 d the Common Good.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270713@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270904T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270904T011200
SUMMARY:Holding is not owning
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nTo hold something in attention can differ 
 radically from possessing or mastering it.\n\nJulian can hold unresolved c
 ontradiction without rushing to explain it away. Hope is allowed to be fir
 mer than the available theory.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith William Blake (“A w
 orld fits a grain”): Julian's hazelnut and Blake's grain of sand both le
 t the minute open onto the cosmic. Their metaphysics differ\, but the chan
 ge of scale is striking.\n\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“Name the thought-pa
 ttern”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The useful fric
 tion is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other asks what h
 ad been filtering perception in the first place. Julian of Norwich's route
  is visionary theology of creation\, suffering\, and love\; Evagrius Ponti
 cus's is ascetic psychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts.\n\nSou
 rce: hazelnut vision.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270714@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270905T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270905T011200
SUMMARY:Form can think
DESCRIPTION:George Herbert\n\nThe shape\, rhythm\, and arrangement of langu
 age can carry an argument that paraphrase alone cannot preserve.\n\nHerber
 t makes ordinary objects into conceptual instruments: a pulley\, collar\, 
 altar\, window\, or flower can carry an argument.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Ja
 cques Maritain (“The person exceeds the role”): Maritain's claim that 
 art knows through making becomes concrete in Herbert\, where poetic form d
 oes intellectual work paraphrase cannot replace.\n\nQUOTATION\n“Love bad
 e me welcome: yet my soul drew back.” — George Herbert\n\nSource: patt
 ern poems and devotional poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270715@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270906T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270906T011200
SUMMARY:Limits make a shape
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nA boundary can create the conditions for fo
 rm\, play\, and meaning rather than merely restricting them.\n\nChesterton
  uses paradox to make the overfamiliar strange enough to see again.\n\nCON
 VERSATION\nWith George MacDonald (“Fantasy can tell truth”): Chesterto
 n inherits much from MacDonald's imaginative Christianity: wonder is not c
 hildish simplification but a discipline for seeing the ordinary as conting
 ent and strange.\n\nWith William Blake (“A world fits a grain”): Blake
  and Chesterton use paradox to dislodge the anesthetic of habit. Blake's r
 esult is visionary revolt\; Chesterton's\, renewed astonishment at existen
 ce.\n\nSource: Orthodoxy and essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270716@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270907T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270907T011200
SUMMARY:The false self performs
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nA socially manufactured identity can become so
  fluent that its performance is mistaken for the whole person.\n\nMerton's
  false self is not merely dishonesty. It is the identity assembled from st
 atus\, comparison\, performance\, and reflected approval.\n\nSource: New S
 eeds of Contemplation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270717@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270908T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270908T011200
SUMMARY:A merciful heart widens
DESCRIPTION:Isaac of Nineveh\n\nCompassion can expand beyond one's preferre
 d circle until alien suffering becomes difficult to exclude.\n\nHis mercif
 ul heart widens until even animal suffering and the suffering of enemies b
 ecome difficult to exclude.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Julian of Norwich (“Ho
 lding is not owning”): Julian and Isaac share a startling confidence in 
 mercy without making suffering imaginary. Both press compassion beyond ord
 inary punitive accounting.\n\nSource: Ascetical Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270718@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270909T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270909T011200
SUMMARY:Resistance can educate
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA difficulty can become diagnostic: not be
 cause suffering is inherently good\, but because resistance reveals where 
 freedom is thin.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Jacques Maritain (“The person exc
 eeds the role”): Freedom is not external to identity\; repeated choices 
 participate in building the self that will make the next choice.\n\nSource
 : sayings tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270719@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270910T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270910T011200
SUMMARY:Simplicity removes excuses
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA stripped-down environment reduces the nu
 mber of external explanations available for one's own agitation.\n\nDistin
 ction: Solitude is not automatically freedom from distraction. Sometimes i
 t only removes the objects onto which distraction was projected.\n\nSource
 : desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270720@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270911T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270911T011200
SUMMARY:Desire can argue with itself
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\n\nA poem can let conflicting desires speak 
 without immediately resolving which voice is the 'real' one.\n\nObservatio
 n: Rossetti's restraint often concentrates pressure. What is withheld beco
 mes structurally present in the poem.\n\nSource: poetry.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270721@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270912T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270912T011200
SUMMARY:Light can blind
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nToo much light can be phenomenologically eq
 uivalent to darkness when the perceiver cannot accommodate its intensity.\
 n\nImage: Too much light can look like darkness when the eye lacks the cap
 acity to receive it.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Christina Rossetti (“Desire c
 an argue with itself”): Read today's thought backward through Christina 
 Rossetti: “Desire can argue with itself.” Desire continues where compr
 ehension fails\; not-knowing becomes a condition of relation rather than a
  reason to stop.\n\nSource: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270722@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270913T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270913T011200
SUMMARY:Experience has texture
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nSpiritual language becomes concrete in the
  Macarian tradition: warmth\, fire\, sweetness\, struggle\, weight\, and m
 ovement.\n\nThe Macarian tradition permits contraries to coexist within on
 e person without pretending either side is unreal.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith P
 seudo-Dionysius (“Light can blind”): Language reaches its limit withou
 t becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give 
 way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270723@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270914T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270914T011200
SUMMARY:Vision changes objects
DESCRIPTION:William Blake\n\nThe world encountered is partly shaped by the 
 quality and habits of perception brought to it.\n\nBlake distrusts any sys
 tem that becomes a substitute for vision. The danger is not thought but th
 ought hardened into a cage for perception.\n\nSource: prophetic writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270724@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270915T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270915T011200
SUMMARY:The cosmos is relational
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nCreated things have meaning not only a
 s isolated entities but through the relations that gather them into a larg
 er order.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Isaac of Nineveh (“A merciful heart wide
 ns”): Isaac's widening mercy and Maximus's cosmic communion meet in a re
 fusal to let spiritual life end at the boundary of the private self.\n\nSo
 urce: Ambigua.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270725@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270916T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270916T011200
SUMMARY:Means educate desire
DESCRIPTION:Jacques Maritain\n\nThe means used toward an end reshape the ag
 ent pursuing it\, and can quietly alter the end itself.\n\nHis distinction
 s are meant to preserve complexity without breaking the world into unrelat
 ed compartments.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Anthony the Great (“Resistance ca
 n educate”): Freedom is not external to identity\; repeated choices part
 icipate in building the self that will make the next choice.\n\nSource: po
 litical and moral philosophy.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270726@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270917T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270917T011200
SUMMARY:Experience needs discernment
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nIntensity does not authenticate an experien
 ce. Teresa repeatedly tests experiences by their effects and coherence.\n\
 nTeresa treats prayer more as relation than technique. Friendship changes 
 the logic from performance to presence.\n\nSource: Life\; Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270727@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270918T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270918T011200
SUMMARY:The toy is double
DESCRIPTION:Eugen Fink\n\nA toy is both an ordinary object and\, within pla
 y\, something transformed by the world the players inhabit.\n\nFink's grea
 t existential phenomena—play\, love\, death\, work\, domination—are wa
 ys the world becomes intelligible through lived structures.\n\nSource: Pla
 y as Symbol of the World.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270728@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270919T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270919T011200
SUMMARY:Wonder needs familiarity
DESCRIPTION:G. K. Chesterton\n\nThe ordinary can become astonishing when fa
 miliar existence is seen as something that need not have been.\n\nHis defe
 nse of limits is playful rather than cramped: boundaries create the field 
 in which a form or game becomes possible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Pseudo-Dio
 nysius (“Light can blind”): Language reaches its limit without becomin
 g useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must give way to un
 saying.\n\nQUOTATION\n“The world will never starve for want of wonders\;
  but only for want of wonder.” — G. K. Chesterton\n\nSource: Orthodoxy
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270729@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270920T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270920T011200
SUMMARY:The shadow has news
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nRejected psychic material often returns indirectly
 . What disturbs the self-image may contain information the self needs.\n\n
 For Jung\, a symbol is alive precisely when it exceeds the explanation cur
 rently available for it.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Teresa of Ávila (“Experi
 ence needs discernment”): Jung and Teresa both imagine interior life spa
 tially. Teresa's castle is theological and ordered toward prayer\; Jung's 
 psyche is analytic and symbolic. The shared architecture makes their diffe
 rence more interesting.\n\nSource: shadow and individuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270730@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270921T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270921T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can deepen mystery
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nGreater knowledge need not reduce mystery. 
 It can increase awareness of what exceeds knowledge.\n\nSource: Life of Mo
 ses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270731@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270922T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270922T011200
SUMMARY:Small acts expose worlds
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nA seemingly minor practice can reveal the 
 structure of desires that abstract reflection leaves hidden.\n\nMacarius i
 magines the heart as a landscape with rooms\, depths\, conflicts\, and cap
 acities not yet integrated.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Maximus the Confessor (
 “The cosmos is relational”): Both resist the isolated unit. Form emerg
 es from relations\, while relation becomes perceptible through form.\n\nSo
 urce: Macarian tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270801@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270923T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270923T011200
SUMMARY:Prayer can be practical
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nContemplative practice is tested in ordinar
 y relations\, not merely in intensity of experience.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith
  Carl Jung (“The shadow has news”): Jung and Teresa both imagine inter
 ior life spatially. Teresa's castle is theological and ordered toward pray
 er\; Jung's psyche is analytic and symbolic. The shared architecture makes
  their difference more interesting.\n\nSource: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270802@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270924T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270924T011200
SUMMARY:Good form has roughness
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\n\nExact regularity can weaken life when 
 it suppresses local adaptation. Roughness can be evidence of responsive or
 der.\n\nAlexander distrusts designs that are correct in isolation but dead
  in context. The unit of judgment is usually the relation\, not the object
  by itself.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Eugen Fink (“The toy is double”): Fi
 nk's world-horizon and Alexander's field of centers resist treating things
  as isolated units. Each asks what larger order lets a thing be what it is
 .\n\nSource: The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270803@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270925T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270925T011200
SUMMARY:Habits travel with you
DESCRIPTION:Anthony the Great\n\nA new place cannot by itself remove the pa
 tterns one carries into it.\n\nStaying put becomes diagnostic when novelty
  has been functioning as a reset button for the same unresolved pattern.\n
 \nSource: desert tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270804@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270926T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270926T011200
SUMMARY:Confession changes memory
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nRecounting a life can reorganize the mean
 ing of past events without changing the events themselves.\n\nAugustine's 
 self is intimate but not transparent. Memory is closer than almost anythin
 g\, yet it exceeds the mind's ability to survey it.\n\nSource: Confessions
 .
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270805@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270927T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270927T011200
SUMMARY:Wisdom may look comic
DESCRIPTION:Abba Moses\n\nA deliberately absurd gesture can carry moral for
 ce when it disarms abstraction and makes a hidden contradiction visible.\n
 \nAbba Moses often teaches by concrete gesture rather than abstract maxim.
  The comic image does philosophical work.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Gregory of
  Nyssa (“Knowing can deepen mystery”): Language reaches its limit with
 out becoming useless. The interesting point is where saying truly must giv
 e way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Sayings of the Desert Fathers.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270806@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270928T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270928T011200
SUMMARY:Contemplation unmasks roles
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nSilence can expose how much identity depends o
 n rehearsed roles and reflected approval.\n\nSource: New Seeds of Contempl
 ation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270807@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270929T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270929T011200
SUMMARY:Difference survives union
DESCRIPTION:Maximus the Confessor\n\nThe strongest union is not fusion. Gen
 uine communion preserves what makes the participants distinct.\n\nParadox:
  The deepest union may preserve distinction more fiercely than fusion does
 .\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Teresa of Ávila (“Prayer can be practical”): 
 Both thoughts resist unity by erasure. Relation becomes stronger when diff
 erence survives contact. Maximus the Confessor's route is cosmic relation\
 , difference\, and communion\; Teresa of Ávila's is interior architecture
 \, prayer\, and discernment.\n\nWith Christopher Alexander (“Good form h
 as roughness”): Alexander's wholeness and Maximus's communion share a st
 ructural intuition: unity is strongest when differences are related withou
 t being flattened.\n\nSource: Christological and cosmic theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270808@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270930T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270930T011200
SUMMARY:Negation is not nihilism
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius\n\nSaying what cannot be said is not the same 
 as saying there is nothing there.\n\nDistinction: Apophatic negation is no
 t saying 'nothing is there.' It refuses to let finite predicates pretend t
 o contain what they signify.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Abba Moses (“Wisdom m
 ay look comic”): Language reaches its limit without becoming useless. Th
 e interesting point is where saying truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSo
 urce: Mystical Theology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270809@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271001T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271001T011200
SUMMARY:Images can recruit desire
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nMental images can carry affective force th
 at exceeds their apparent informational content.\n\nEvagrius treats though
 ts less as private opinions than as recurring patterns with recognizable s
 equences and effects.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (“Contemplatio
 n unmasks roles”): Both thoughts alter the conditions of noticing. The u
 seful friction is that one asks what becomes perceptible while the other a
 sks what had been filtering perception in the first place. Evagrius Pontic
 us's route is ascetic psychology and the analysis of recurring thoughts\; 
 Thomas Merton's is contemplation\, the false self\, and social presence.\n
 \nSource: ascetical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270810@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271002T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271002T011200
SUMMARY:Virtue has no ceiling
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nMoral growth has no final plateau where fur
 ther transformation becomes unnecessary.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Maximus the
  Confessor (“Difference survives union”): Both resist the isolated uni
 t. Form emerges from relations\, while relation becomes perceptible throug
 h form.\n\nSource: Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270811@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271003T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271003T011200
SUMMARY:The path keeps opening
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\n\nProgress can reveal new distances rather th
 an closing them\, without making the journey futile.\n\nSource: Life of Mo
 ses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270812@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271004T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271004T011200
SUMMARY:Obscurity can be honest
DESCRIPTION:The Cloud author\n\nNot understanding can be a more truthful st
 ance than pretending an experience has become conceptually manageable.\n\n
 Unknowing here is disciplined rather than vague: the mind relinquishes the
  demand to convert encounter immediately into possession.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith Pseudo-Dionysius (“Negation is not nihilism”): Both thoughts mak
 e intellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The 
 question is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or trans
 formed desire. The Cloud author's route is apophatic contemplation and the
  discipline of unknowing\; Pseudo-Dionysius's is apophatic theology and sy
 mbolic language.\n\nSource: The Cloud of Unknowing.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270813@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271005T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271005T011200
SUMMARY:Hope disputes the present
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\n\nHope is not optimism about present trends. 
 It lets a promised future contradict what the present declares inevitable.
 \n\nSource: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270814@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271006T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271006T011200
SUMMARY:Tension can make form
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nOpposition and contraction need not be mere def
 ects. Tension can participate in the emergence of form.\n\nLight\, for Bö
 hme\, needs distinction in order to appear as anything at all.\n\nCONVERSA
 TION\nWith Maximus the Confessor (“Difference survives union”): Both r
 esist the isolated unit. Form emerges from relations\, while relation beco
 mes perceptible through form.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270815@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271007T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271007T011200
SUMMARY:The end rereads the start
DESCRIPTION:Origen\n\nA vision of final restoration can alter how creation\
 , judgment\, and history are understood from the beginning.\n\nDistinction
 : Multiple senses do not mean anything goes. Origen's richer readings rema
 in ordered toward transformation and coherence.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The 
 Cloud author (“Obscurity can be honest”): An image can hold what a con
 cept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way of remaining precise without
  forcing closure.\n\nSource: On First Principles.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270816@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271008T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271008T011200
SUMMARY:Difference can reveal unity
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nThe coincidence of opposites does not erase
  finite distinctions\; it changes the level at which their opposition is i
 nterpreted.\n\nCusa uses precision to discover the limits of precision. Be
 tter measurement can increase knowledge and clarify what measurement canno
 t reach.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith The Cloud author (“Obscurity can be hones
 t”): Both thoughts make intellectual limitation productive rather than m
 erely embarrassing. The question is whether the limit invites silence\, be
 tter concepts\, or transformed desire. Nicholas of Cusa's route is learned
  ignorance\, infinity\, and mathematical metaphor\; The Cloud author's is 
 apophatic contemplation and the discipline of unknowing.\n\nWith Gregory o
 f Nyssa (“Virtue has no ceiling”): Gregory's endless progress and Cusa
 's learned ignorance both let greater knowledge disclose a greater remaind
 er. Progress is real\, but completion recedes for principled reasons.\n\nS
 ource: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270817@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271009T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271009T011200
SUMMARY:A thought has a sequence
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\n\nTempting thoughts often develop in stages\
 ; recognizing an early stage can matter more than winning an argument afte
 r fixation.\n\nEvagrius treats thoughts less as private opinions than as r
 ecurring patterns with recognizable sequences and effects.\n\nCONVERSATION
 \nWith Nicholas of Cusa (“Difference can reveal unity”): Language reac
 hes its limit without becoming useless. The interesting point is where say
 ing truly must give way to unsaying.\n\nSource: Praktikos.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270818@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271010T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271010T011200
SUMMARY:Hope can remain unfinished
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nJulian's confidence does not require a com
 plete theory of how every contradiction will be resolved.\n\nCONVERSATION\
 nWith Jürgen Moltmann (“Hope disputes the present”): Moltmann and Jul
 ian let hope resist the apparent finality of suffering\, but neither needs
  to pretend the wound is unreal.\n\nQUOTATION\n“All shall be well.” 
 — Julian of Norwich\n\nSource: Revelations.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270819@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271011T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271011T011200
SUMMARY:The world is not elsewhere
DESCRIPTION:Thomas Merton\n\nContemplation is not necessarily escape from t
 he world\; it can intensify participation in what is actually present.\n\n
 Merton's silence can become social. It is valuable when it makes the world
  more perceptible\, not when it becomes a private spiritual possession.\n\
 nCONVERSATION\nWith Nicholas of Cusa (“Difference can reveal unity”): 
 An image can hold what a concept cannot finish. Imagination becomes a way 
 of remaining precise without forcing closure.\n\nSource: later writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270820@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271012T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271012T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness keeps tension
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nWholeness is not spotless consistency but a capaci
 ty to hold previously disowned tensions without simple collapse.\n\nJung t
 reats disturbance as potentially informative. A symptom\, projection\, or 
 dream may be carrying material the conscious attitude has refused to host.
 \n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“A thought has a sequence”):
  Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different ps
 ychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of aut
 onomy rather than transparent expressions of consciousness.\n\nSource: ind
 ividuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270821@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271013T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271013T011200
SUMMARY:Persona is a useful mask
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\n\nA social persona is necessary\, but trouble begins
  when the mask is mistaken for the whole person.\n\nFor Jung\, a symbol is
  alive precisely when it exceeds the explanation currently available for i
 t.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Evagrius Ponticus (“A thought has a sequence”
 ): Jung's complexes and Evagrius's recurring thoughts belong to different 
 psychologies\, yet both treat mental events as patterns with a degree of a
 utonomy rather than transparent expressions of consciousness.\n\nSource: T
 wo Essays on Analytical Psychology.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270822@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271014T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271014T011200
SUMMARY:Loss can simplify love
DESCRIPTION:John of the Cross\n\nWhen supports disappear\, desire can becom
 e more exact about what it actually seeks.\n\nJohn keeps asking what desir
 e actually seeks after familiar consolation is removed.\n\nCONVERSATION\nW
 ith Nicholas of Cusa (“Difference can reveal unity”): Both thoughts ma
 ke intellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The
  question is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or tran
 sformed desire. John of the Cross's route is dark night\, detachment\, and
  transformed desire\; Nicholas of Cusa's is learned ignorance\, infinity\,
  and mathematical metaphor.\n\nSource: Dark Night.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270823@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271015T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271015T011200
SUMMARY:The self is a landscape
DESCRIPTION:Macarius of Egypt\n\nInner life can be spatially imagined: room
 s\, depths\, battlefields\, hidden regions\, and places not yet explored.\
 n\nDistinction: Inner conflict does not prove one side unreal. Contraries 
 can genuinely share the same person.\n\nSource: Macarian Homilies.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270824@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271016T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271016T011200
SUMMARY:Harmony keeps tension
DESCRIPTION:Jakob Böhme\n\nHarmony need not mean the absence of opposition
 \; it can mean oppositions held in a generative order.\n\nBöhme thinks dy
 namically: manifestation requires difference\, resistance\, and tension ra
 ther than inert substance alone.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Thomas Merton (“T
 he world is not elsewhere”): Desire colors the image\, while the image e
 ducates desire. Each can quietly teach the other what to want.\n\nWith Joh
 n of the Cross (“Loss can simplify love”): Desire changes when its obj
 ect cannot be conceptually possessed. Not-knowing can purify longing or ma
 ke it more exact.\n\nSource: theosophical writings.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270825@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271017T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271017T011200
SUMMARY:Can the game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nSome games seek a terminating victory\; other
 s are played so that possibility and participation can continue.\n\nCarse 
 is not contrasting seriousness with frivolity. His real axis is closure ve
 rsus continuation: does the activity aim to end the field\, or keep genuin
 e possibility alive?\n\nQUOTATION\nCarse's governing distinction: finite g
 ames aim at winning\; infinite games at continuing the play.\n\nSource: Fi
 nite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270826@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271018T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271018T011200
SUMMARY:Beware tidy goodness
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\n\nMoral self-certainty can become a way of
  avoiding conversion.\n\nO'Connor keeps the comic and the terrible close t
 ogether. Laughter can expose self-deception more efficiently than solemn m
 oral instruction.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith Carl Jung (“Wholeness keeps tens
 ion”): Read today's thought backward through Carl Jung: “Persona is a 
 useful mask.” Both refuse the transparent self\; identity contains rooms
 \, masks\, memories\, habits\, or forces the narrator does not fully comma
 nd.\n\nSource: fiction and essays.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270827@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271019T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271019T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing has an asymptote
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\n\nKnowledge can approach its object more exac
 tly while simultaneously clarifying the distance that remains.\n\nLearned 
 ignorance is intellectual achievement\, not surrender: the knower becomes 
 more exact about the limits of the knowing instrument.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWi
 th John of the Cross (“Loss can simplify love”): Both thoughts make in
 tellectual limitation productive rather than merely embarrassing. The ques
 tion is whether the limit invites silence\, better concepts\, or transform
 ed desire. Nicholas of Cusa's route is learned ignorance\, infinity\, and 
 mathematical metaphor\; John of the Cross's is dark night\, detachment\, a
 nd transformed desire.\n\nSource: On Learned Ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270828@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271020T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271020T011200
SUMMARY:Entertainment can refuse
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\n\nA performer can use the stage to refuse the aud
 ience's preferred contract rather than satisfy it.\n\nHe treats entertainm
 ent's social contract as something that can itself be performed\, broken\,
  or made visible.\n\nCONVERSATION\nWith James P. Carse (“Can the game st
 ay open?”): Carse's open play clarifies something in Kaufman: the strong
 est performance often leaves the audience without a final verdict about wh
 at game was being played.\n\nSource: performance practice.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270829@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271021T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271021T011200
SUMMARY:The castle has rooms
DESCRIPTION:Teresa of Ávila\n\nDifferent modes of awareness can coexist wi
 thin one person without all being equally accessible at once.\n\nImage: Th
 e self is a castle with rooms inside rooms\; familiarity with the front ha
 ll is not knowledge of the whole building.\n\nSource: Interior Castle.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270830@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271022T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271022T011200
SUMMARY:Vulnerability can endure
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\n\nThe created thing is fragile\, contingent\
 , and still held in being\; dependence is not identical with worthlessness
 .\n\nJulian's confidence does not come from denying wounds. Her distinctiv
 e move is to deny them the final interpretive authority.\n\nSource: hazeln
 ut vision.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20270831@1am-wisdom-v2
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271023T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271023T011200
SUMMARY:The self is not transparent
DESCRIPTION:Augustine of Hippo\n\nIntrospection does not make the self full
 y available to itself. Memory and motive exceed conscious inspection.\n\nO
 bservation: Augustine's account of time is intimate: past and future becom
 e present as remembering and expecting.\n\nSource: Confessions.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wisdom-annual-coda-20271024@1am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20261024T060000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20271024T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20271024T011200
SUMMARY:Play can begin again
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\n\nAn infinite game does not begin anew by erasi
 ng what has happened. It begins again by refusing to let a previous ending
  govern every possible next move.\n\nConversation: Augustine's account of 
 a self not fully transparent to itself makes this more difficult. If we ca
 rry our unexamined past into every new game\, beginning again is not forge
 tting\; it is leaving room for that past to become newly intelligible.\n\n
 Source: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
STATUS:CONFIRMED
RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=365
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
